#!/usr/bin/env python3
# aegis-summary: Captures and restores the tenants' DATA
# aegis-group:   backup
# aegis-subcommands: backup list restore size remote
"""aegis-data — captures and restores the tenants' DATA.

It is the other half of `aegis state backup`. That one backs up the
platform's STATE —the store's secrets, the init's trail, the edge's
tfstate—; this one backs up what the tenants GENERATED: their databases
and their objects. Neither half is any good alone: with the state you
raise an empty platform, with the data you have a dump nobody knows where
to put.

    aegis data backup                    # one bundle PER ORGANIZATION
    aegis data list <bundle.age>         # what is inside, without restoring
    aegis data restore <bundle.age> --org ejemplo [--force]
    aegis data size [--org ejemplo]      # what it weighs, as metrics

── ONE BUNDLE PER ORGANIZATION, IN ITS OWN FOLDER ──────────────────

    $AEGIS_BACKUPS/
      .aegis-destino                       <- the disk's mark (see below)
      platform/
        aegis-state-<ts>.age              <- written by aegis state backup
      org-ejemplo/
        aegis-datos-org-ejemplo-<ts>.age
      org-portafolio/
        aegis-datos-org-portafolio-<ts>.age

Until 2026-08-11 it was ONE bundle with everything inside. Separating it
per organization changes three things that matter:

  · restoring one does not force you to open everybody else's
    safekeeping — and that safekeeping carries third parties' data;
  · decommissioning an organization is deleting its folder, not editing a
    shared file;
  · and the one that really weighs: the honest question stops being "is
    there a backup?" and becomes "does EVERY organization have a backup?".

That last one is the underlying reason. With a single bundle, a new
organization that was never backed up was INVISIBLE: the file existed, it
had a recent date, and it was not inside. Now the absence has the shape
of a missing folder, and `aegis check` enumerates it against the
contracts.

── THE DISK'S MARK (.aegis-destino) ────────────────────────────────

The typical destination is a second disk, and on a desktop that disk is
usually mounted by the graphical environment at login — it is not in
fstab and it is NOT mounted during boot. If the backup runs in that gap,
the path exists all the same (it is an empty directory on the root disk)
and the bundle is written there: success reported, zero protection, and
the copy that was supposed to survive the disk lives on the disk.

That is why the first capture notes the destination filesystem's UUID
down in `<root>/.aegis-destino`, and the following ones verify it. If it
does not match —it is not mounted, or it is another disk— NOTHING is
written. The UUID is compared and not the mount point because the mount
point is exactly what lies in this failure.

── WHY IT RUNS ON THE OPERATOR'S SIDE AND NOT INSIDE THE CLUSTER ───

It is not a preference: it is the only thing the platform allows without
loosening it. Measured on 2026-08-09:

  1. The namespaces with data are ALL `restricted` (org-*, garage-system,
     ai-system). A Job there CANNOT mount a hostPath, so it cannot write
     into the backups directory.
  2. The egress of a tenant namespace is closed except for DNS. A pod in
     there can dump the database —`allow-intra-namespace` allows it— but
     it cannot GET the dump OUT over the network.
  3. Garage is distroless: no shell. Its backup goes through the S3 API,
     not through the filesystem.
  4. /var/lib/rancher/k3s/storage is not readable without root, and its
     paths carry the PVC's UUID — which on a new instance is ANOTHER one.
     That is, restoring cannot be "copy the directory back" either.

The alternative —a CronJob inside— demands a lax namespace, one network
rule per tenant and a permanent identity able to read every database.
That is a target, and it is a lot of machinery for a one-node platform.
Here the transport is `kubectl exec` (verified without loss: 5136 bytes
inside, 5136 on the host) and `kubectl port-forward` for S3, which
besides dodges the NetworkPolicy legitimately because the traffic is born
on the node.

For the SCHEDULED mode, this is fired from a host timer. It is more
honest than a CronJob: the one that backs up does not live inside what it
backs up.

── WHAT IS CAPTURED AND HOW, WHICH IS WHERE THE CORRECTNESS IS ─────

postgres  `pg_dumpall`, NOT `pg_dump`. Measured: the ROLES live outside
          any database, and a normal `pg_dump` loses them. Restoring
          without them gives a database with no owners and an app that
          cannot get in. `pg_dumpall` also brings the OTHER databases
          (there is a `postgres` one besides the contract's).

bucket    Listing and download over S3 with a SigV4 signature. The object
          travels with its key and its sha256; on restoring, the sha256
          is re-verified before writing and the size after.

THE PASSWORD IS NOT COPIED INTO THE BUNDLE, its FINGERPRINT is stored.
The globals dump already brings each role's SCRAM hash, so the restored
database accepts the password THAT WAS THERE AT CAPTURE TIME. That is
right on the machine that took the capture and wrong on any other one:
the new instance generated its own credential, the dump knows nothing
about it, and the database ends up expecting one password while the pod
presents another. The manifest therefore stores the sha256 of the one
that was there —`restore` COMPARES, and a difference is worth a person,
because restoring a machine is not the same operation as moving an
organization into a new one— and once the dump is loaded, `restore`
REALIGNS the role with the live Secret and proves that the credential
opens the database. Copying the secret into the bundle would have hidden
the discrepancy by creating it in silence; storing the fingerprint
detects it, and realigning closes it.

── WHAT IT WEIGHS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

The instance's storage class is local-path: it does not measure or limit
the disk a PVC really uses, so the cluster cannot answer how much an
organization weighs — what `kubectl` returns is the size that was
REQUESTED. `size` asks the ones that do know (postgres for its
databases, the bucket listing for its objects) and prints `aegis_org_*`
series on stdout, in the same format every other producer on this
platform speaks. It pushes nothing: where those series go is decided by
whoever calls it.
"""
import argparse
import base64
import datetime
import getpass
import hashlib
import hmac
import json
import os
import re
import secrets as onetime
import shutil
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import tarfile
import tempfile
import time
import urllib.error
import urllib.parse
import urllib.request
import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET

try:
    import yaml
except ImportError:
    sys.exit("pyyaml is missing (python3-yaml)")

# ── canonical python preamble (V-102) ────────────────────────────────
# The product comes out of the variable (bin/aegis exports it) or out of
# this file; the INSTANCE is decided by lib/aegis/paths.py and nobody
# else.
_AEGIS_ROOT = os.environ.get("AEGIS_ROOT") or os.path.dirname(
    os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)))
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(_AEGIS_ROOT, "lib"))
from aegis import cli, paths             # noqa: E402

# PLATFORM_DIR is the INSTANCE (the live checkout of the platform), not
# the product. In v2 this line was `dirname(dirname(__file__))` and it
# worked because the command lived INSIDE the instance. On moving the
# code to the product the same line went on compiling and started
# pointing at the wrong place — C1/C2's «dependency invisible to a grep».
PLATFORM_DIR = str(paths.platform_dir())
ORGS_DIR = os.path.join(PLATFORM_DIR, "orgs")
SERVICES = os.path.join(PLATFORM_DIR, "services.yaml")
DEFAULT_DEST = os.environ.get(
    "AEGIS_BACKUPS", os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), "aegis-backups"))

red = "\033[31m"; green = "\033[32m"; yellow = "\033[33m"; gray = "\033[90m"; reset = "\033[0m"
if not sys.stdout.isatty():
    red = green = yellow = gray = reset = ""


def die(msg):
    print(f"{red}✗{reset} {msg}", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(1)


# ── the destination: that it be the disk we believe ─────────────────
def uuid_of(path):
    """UUID of the filesystem where `path` lives (or None if it cannot be read)."""
    r = subprocess.run(["findmnt", "-no", "UUID", "--target", path],
                       capture_output=True, text=True)
    return r.stdout.strip() or None


def prepare_root(root):
    """Creates the backups root and verifies that it is the usual disk.

    The failure this prevents is not theoretical and does not announce
    itself: a second disk mounted by the desktop is not mounted during
    boot, the path goes on existing as an empty directory of the root
    disk, and the backup lands there believing it went out. It is
    discovered the day it is needed.
    """
    root = os.path.abspath(os.path.expanduser(root))
    os.makedirs(root, exist_ok=True)
    # `.aegis-destino` and the `uuid` field are the on-disk FORMAT:
    # aegis-check reads this same file to tell whether the disk is the
    # one that was noted down (it reads `uuid` and nothing else).
    # Renaming THOSE TWO is a coordinated change between the two
    # commands and every disk already in use, not a translation.
    # The other two fields are for whoever opens the file with an
    # editor the day something does not add up, so they are prose and
    # they are written in the same language as the rest of the product.
    mark = os.path.join(root, ".aegis-destino")
    current = uuid_of(root)
    if current is None:
        print(f"  {yellow}!{reset} I could not read the UUID of the filesystem of {root} "
              f"(findmnt?): I go on, but WITHOUT the disk guard")
        return root
    if os.path.exists(mark):
        try:
            prev = json.load(open(mark, encoding="utf-8"))
        except Exception:
            prev = {}
        noted = prev.get("uuid")
        if noted and noted != current:
            die(
                f"the backups destination changed DISK and nothing is written.\n"
                f"      noted down : {noted}\n"
                f"      now        : {current}\n"
                f"      {root}\n"
                f"      The usual thing is that the backups disk is not mounted and\n"
                f"      this path is an empty directory of the root disk — that is, the\n"
                f"      safekeeping would fall on the same disk it has to survive.\n"
                f"      Mount it and repeat. If the change is on purpose, delete\n"
                f"      {mark} and the next capture notes it down again.")
    else:
        json.dump({
            "uuid": current,
            "noted_at": datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc)
                        .strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ"),
            "why": "if this UUID does not match, the backups disk is not "
                   "mounted and the bundle would land on the very disk it "
                   "has to survive",
        }, open(mark, "w", encoding="utf-8"), indent=2, ensure_ascii=False)
        ok(f"destination noted down: {root} (fs {current})")
    if uuid_of("/") == current:
        print(f"  {yellow}!{reset} the destination is on the SAME filesystem as / — a "
              f"failure of that disk takes the data and its backups with it")
    return root


def step(msg):
    print(f"  {gray}·{reset} {msg}")


def ok(msg):
    print(f"  {green}✓{reset} {msg}")


def kubectl(args, binary=False, stdin_data=None):
    r = subprocess.run(["kubectl"] + args, capture_output=True,
                       input=stdin_data, **({} if binary else {"text": True}))
    return r


def secret_value(ns, secret, key):
    """One key of a Secret, in bytes. It is used, never printed.

    There is ONE door and it returns bytes: whoever calls it decides what
    to do with them, and nobody has the excuse of dumping the whole
    object with `-o yaml` to see what is inside. It exists because the
    restore needs the credential OF THE INSTANCE IT IS RESTORING INTO —
    the bundle deliberately carries none.
    """
    r = kubectl(["get", "secret", secret, "-n", ns, "-o",
                 "jsonpath={.data." + key + "}"])
    if r.returncode != 0 or not r.stdout.strip():
        return None
    return base64.b64decode(r.stdout.strip())


def shq(value):
    """A shell argument, quoted.

    Nothing that goes through here is a secret —a secret does not travel
    in argv, see `realign_role`—: what this protects is the OTHER layer.
    What the pod runs is a `sh -c` script, and into that script go the
    role and the database name. The role comes out of a contract, which
    is text a person writes; the database name comes out of the LIVE
    SERVER, where the tenant —POSTGRES_USER is the superuser of its own
    instance— can create one with whatever name it likes. `sql_literal`
    and `sql_identifier` protect the SQL; this protects the shell that
    carries it.
    """
    return "'" + value.replace("'", "'\\''") + "'"


def sql_identifier(name):
    return '"' + name.replace('"', '""') + '"'


def sql_literal(value):
    """A SQL string literal.

    With `standard_conforming_strings` —on by default since 9.1, and the
    engine here is 17— the backslash is an ordinary character, so
    doubling the quote is the whole escape.
    """
    return "'" + value.replace("'", "''") + "'"


# ── discovery ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The same as aegis registry and for the same reason: the list is NOT
# written. It comes out of the contracts and is CONTRASTED against the
# cluster. A source that is on one side and not on the other is exactly
# the condition that makes a backup dangerous —capturing too little
# without finding out— so it is an error and not a warning.
# The stateful types the catalogue knows how to dump. Postgres has its
# own bespoke path in this file —globals apart, one file per database,
# the --clean lesson— and mongodb (and anything added later with
# `component: datos` and `backup.method: dump`) rides the GENERIC path,
# which runs the very commands services.yaml carries. The set is DERIVED
# from the catalogue, not listed here: a type whose backup nobody wrote
# does not become a silent gap, it simply is not a source.
def _dump_types():
    cat = (yaml.safe_load(open(SERVICES, encoding="utf-8")) or {}).get("tipos") or {}
    out = {}
    for name, spec in cat.items():
        b = (spec or {}).get("backup") or {}
        if (spec or {}).get("component") == "datos" and b.get("method") == "dump":
            out[name] = b
    return out


def sources():
    databases, mongos, buckets = [], [], []
    dump_types = _dump_types()
    for f in sorted(os.listdir(ORGS_DIR)):
        if not f.endswith(".yaml"):
            continue
        d = yaml.safe_load(open(os.path.join(ORGS_DIR, f), encoding="utf-8"))
        org = d.get("organizacion") or d.get("nombre")
        ns = f"org-{org}"
        for s in d.get("servicios", []) or []:
            tipo = s.get("tipo")
            if tipo == "postgres":
                databases.append({"tipo": "postgres", "org": org, "ns": ns,
                                  "servicio": s["nombre"], "pod": f"{s['nombre']}-0",
                                  "secreto": f"{s['nombre']}-credenciales"})
            elif tipo in dump_types:
                # mongodb and its kin: the templates travel WITH the piece,
                # resolved for this org and service, so capture and restore
                # run exactly what the catalogue declares and this file
                # never hard-codes a mongo command.
                b = dump_types[tipo]
                subs = {"<org>": org, "<service>": s["nombre"]}
                tmpl = {k: _sub(b[k], subs) for k in ("databases", "dump", "restore") if b.get(k)}
                mongos.append({"tipo": tipo, "org": org, "ns": ns,
                               "servicio": s["nombre"], "pod": f"{s['nombre']}-0",
                               "secreto": f"{s['nombre']}-credenciales", "tmpl": tmpl})
        if (d.get("almacenamiento") or {}).get("bucket"):
            buckets.append({"org": org, "ns": ns, "bucket": org})

    # contrast against the cluster. Every stateful service carries
    # `aegis.dev/component=datos` —postgres AND mongodb— so BOTH have to
    # be in the declared set, or a mongo StatefulSet would read as «alive
    # and undeclared» and abort the backup of every OTHER organization
    # too (measured 2026-09-04, building the shop demo).
    r = kubectl(["get", "statefulset", "-A", "-l", "aegis.dev/component=datos",
                 "--no-headers", "-o",
                 "custom-columns=NS:.metadata.namespace,N:.metadata.name"])
    if r.returncode != 0:
        die("I could not read the cluster's StatefulSets (kubeconfig?)")
    alive = {tuple(l.split()) for l in r.stdout.splitlines() if l.strip()}
    declared = {(b["ns"], b["servicio"]) for b in databases} \
             | {(m["ns"], m["servicio"]) for m in mongos}
    if alive != declared:
        for x in sorted(alive - declared):
            print(f"  {red}!{reset} {x[0]}/{x[1]} lives in the CLUSTER and does not come out of any "
                  f"contract — it would be left WITHOUT a backup")
        for x in sorted(declared - alive):
            print(f"  {red}!{reset} {x[0]}/{x[1]} comes out of a contract and is not in the "
                  f"cluster — either it did not sync, or the contract lies")
        die("the contracts and the cluster do not match: nothing is backed up blind")
    return databases, mongos, buckets


def _sub(tmpl, subs):
    for k, v in subs.items():
        tmpl = tmpl.replace(k, v)
    return tmpl


# ── SigV4 signature, minimal and without dependencies ───────────────
def _sign(key, msg):
    return hmac.new(key, msg.encode(), hashlib.sha256).digest()


def s3(method, host, path, query, ak, sk, region="garage", body=b"",
       scheme="http", timeout=30):
    """One SigV4 signer for the two S3 that this platform speaks.

    Garage lives inside the cluster and is reached over plaintext HTTP
    through a port-forward that never leaves the node; R2 lives on the
    internet and is reached over TLS. That is the WHOLE difference, and
    it is two parameters — the scheme and how long a body may take to
    travel — because the signature is the same protocol on both sides.
    Writing a second signer for the far one would mean two places where
    a canonical request can be got subtly wrong, and the symptom of
    getting it wrong is a 403 that says nothing about which of the two
    is at fault.

    `region` is not decoration either: it goes INTO the credential
    scope of the signature. Garage was configured with `garage` and R2
    demands `auto` — the same key signing with the wrong region gets a
    SignatureDoesNotMatch, which reads exactly like a wrong password.
    """
    t = datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc)
    amz = t.strftime("%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ")
    day = t.strftime("%Y%m%d")
    hpay = hashlib.sha256(body).hexdigest()
    qs = "&".join(f"{urllib.parse.quote(k, safe='')}={urllib.parse.quote(v, safe='')}"
                  for k, v in sorted(query.items()))
    cr = (f"{method}\n{urllib.parse.quote(path)}\n{qs}\n"
          f"host:{host}\nx-amz-content-sha256:{hpay}\nx-amz-date:{amz}\n\n"
          f"host;x-amz-content-sha256;x-amz-date\n{hpay}")
    scope = f"{day}/{region}/s3/aws4_request"
    sts = (f"AWS4-HMAC-SHA256\n{amz}\n{scope}\n"
           f"{hashlib.sha256(cr.encode()).hexdigest()}")
    k = _sign(_sign(_sign(_sign(("AWS4" + sk).encode(), day), region), "s3"), "aws4_request")
    signature = hmac.new(k, sts.encode(), hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()
    headers = {
        "Authorization": (f"AWS4-HMAC-SHA256 Credential={ak}/{scope}, "
                          f"SignedHeaders=host;x-amz-content-sha256;x-amz-date, "
                          f"Signature={signature}"),
        "x-amz-content-sha256": hpay, "x-amz-date": amz, "Host": host,
    }
    url = f"{scheme}://{host}{urllib.parse.quote(path)}" + (f"?{qs}" if qs else "")
    req = urllib.request.Request(url, data=body or None, headers=headers, method=method)
    # The response's headers travel AS THEY COME and are never flattened
    # into a plain dict. HTTP header names are case-insensitive; the
    # mapping urllib returns honours that and a dict does not. Garage is
    # hyper, and hyper writes them in LOWERCASE in HTTP/1.1 unless it is
    # asked for title case, which it is not: measured over the raw
    # `content-length: 17`, `.get("Content-Length", -1)` gives 17 and the
    # dict's gives -1. Through here goes the size the restore reads back
    # from the object it has just uploaded and the ETag that lets it skip
    # what is already identical, so flattening turned every object into
    # «the bucket returns -1 bytes» and every restore of a bucket into a
    # failure. Same door as libexec/aegis-check, which reads `.headers`
    # off the response for the same reason.
    try:
        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as r:
            return r.status, r.read(), r.headers
    except urllib.error.HTTPError as e:
        return e.code, e.read(), e.headers


def s3_credential(ns):
    """The S3 pair of a namespace, out of its live `garage` Secret.

    Capture reads it to take the objects out; restore reads it to put
    them back — and on restore it is decisive that it be THIS instance's
    and not the bundle's: a bundle carries objects, never keys, so
    restoring on a new machine has to sign with the pair that machine
    generated.
    """
    ak = secret_value(ns, "garage", "AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID")
    sk = secret_value(ns, "garage", "AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY")
    if ak is None or sk is None:
        die(f"no S3 credential in {ns}/garage")
    return ak.decode().strip(), sk.decode().strip()


def bucket_listing(host, bucket, ak, sk, scheme="http", region="garage"):
    """Every object in a bucket: its key, its size and its date, paging included.

    ONE lister for the three that need it —capture, restore and the
    measurement—, because the paging of ListObjectsV2 is exactly the kind
    of detail that gets written well once and badly the other two times:
    forgetting the continuation token does not fail, it returns the first
    thousand objects and says nothing.

    The size comes out of the listing itself, which is why asking how much
    a bucket weighs costs one request per thousand objects and not one
    request per object.
    """
    objects, token, go_on = [], None, True
    while go_on:
        q = {"list-type": "2"}
        if token:
            q["continuation-token"] = token
        st, body, _ = s3("GET", host, f"/{bucket}", q, ak, sk,
                         region=region, scheme=scheme)
        if st != 200:
            die(f"listing {bucket} gave HTTP {st}: {body[:200]!r}")
        root = ET.fromstring(body)
        nsx = {"s3": root.tag.split("}")[0].strip("{")} if "}" in root.tag else {}

        def h(n, p_):
            return n.find(f"s3:{p_}", nsx) if nsx else n.find(p_)

        for c in (root.findall("s3:Contents", nsx) if nsx else root.findall("Contents")):
            size = h(c, "Size")
            # LastModified travels because the off-site destination is
            # judged by AGE and by nothing else: «is there a copy?» is
            # answered by any listing, «is the copy from before or after
            # the disaster?» only by its date. It comes out of the
            # listing, which already brings it, and not out of a HEAD per
            # object — that would be one request per bundle to learn what
            # one request per thousand already said.
            when = h(c, "LastModified")
            objects.append({"clave": h(c, "Key").text,
                            "bytes": int(size.text) if size is not None else -1,
                            "modificado": when.text if when is not None else None})
        tr = h(root, "IsTruncated")
        go_on = tr is not None and tr.text == "true"
        nt = h(root, "NextContinuationToken")
        token = nt.text if nt is not None else None
    return objects


class Tunnel:
    """port-forward to Garage's S3.

    It is needed because 3900 only accepts traffic from tenant
    namespaces, and the operator is not one. The port-forward does not
    violate that rule: the traffic is born on the node, which is where the
    one that backs up lives.
    """

    def __init__(self, ns, svc, port):
        self.ns, self.svc, self.port = ns, svc, port
        self.p = None
        self.local = None

    def __enter__(self):
        s = socket.socket(); s.bind(("127.0.0.1", 0))
        self.local = s.getsockname()[1]; s.close()
        self.p = subprocess.Popen(
            ["kubectl", "port-forward", "-n", self.ns, f"svc/{self.svc}",
             f"{self.local}:{self.port}"],
            stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
        for _ in range(50):
            try:
                c = socket.create_connection(("127.0.0.1", self.local), 0.3); c.close()
                return self
            except OSError:
                time.sleep(0.2)
        self.__exit__(None, None, None)
        die(f"the port-forward to {self.ns}/{self.svc}:{self.port} did not come up")

    def __exit__(self, *a):
        if self.p:
            self.p.terminate()
            try:
                self.p.wait(timeout=5)
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                self.p.kill()

    @property
    def host(self):
        return f"127.0.0.1:{self.local}"


# ── capture ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def credential_fingerprint(ns, secret):
    """sha256 of the live password, WITHOUT bringing it into the bundle."""
    v = secret_value(ns, secret, "password")
    return hashlib.sha256(v).hexdigest() if v is not None else None


def psql(ns, pod, database, sql):
    """A one-line query against a database, returns stdout.

    Both interpolations go through `shq`, and the query used to go
    through `json.dumps`. What the pod runs is a `sh -c` script, and
    json.dumps quotes with DOUBLE quotes — inside which the shell still
    expands `$` and the backtick. `sql_literal` protects the SQL; the
    shell is another layer and wants its own quoting.

    It is not hypothetical about the database name: it does not come from
    a contract, it comes from `databases_of()`, that is, from the live
    server. The tenant's application connects as POSTGRES_USER, which is
    the superuser of ITS instance, and can `CREATE DATABASE` with the
    name it likes — a name with `$(...)` would be a command substitution
    inside its own postgres container.
    """
    r = kubectl(["exec", "-n", ns, pod, "--", "sh", "-c",
                 f'psql -U "$POSTGRES_USER" -d {shq(database)} -Atc {shq(sql)}'])
    return (r.stdout or "").strip(), r.returncode


def inventory(ns, pod, database):
    """Tables and rows of a database. It is against this that the restore is verified.

    It is not enough for psql to finish without shouting: the drill of
    2026-08-09 left the database DELETED and the command reported
    success. A restore has to MEASURE what it achieved, not trust its exit
    code.
    """
    out, rc = psql(ns, pod, database,
                   "SELECT schemaname||'.'||relname FROM pg_stat_user_tables ORDER BY 1")
    if rc != 0:
        return None
    tables = []
    for t in [x for x in out.splitlines() if x.strip()]:
        n, rc2 = psql(ns, pod, database, f'SELECT count(*) FROM {t}')
        tables.append({"tabla": t, "filas": int(n) if rc2 == 0 and n.isdigit() else -1})
    return tables


def databases_of(ns, pod):
    out, _ = psql(ns, pod, "postgres",
                  "SELECT datname FROM pg_database WHERE NOT datistemplate "
                  "AND datallowconn ORDER BY 1")
    return [x for x in out.splitlines() if x.strip()]


def capture_database(b, target):
    """Globals apart, and one database per file. The whole pg_dumpall is NOT used.

    Both reasons came out of the drill, and the second nearly broke the
    organization for real:

    1. Without `--clean`, restoring over a database with data DUPLICATES
       it. It nearly went unseen: the first test seemed to go well because
       the table that survived had a primary key and rejected the repeated
       INSERTs. With a table WITHOUT a unique constraint, three rows
       turned into six.

    2. But `pg_dumpall --clean` emits `DROP DATABASE` and `DROP ROLE`, and
       the DROP ROLE of the role you are connected with ALWAYS FAILS. With
       ON_ERROR_STOP=1 that aborts the restore RIGHT AFTER having dropped
       the database: the database disappears and is not recreated. It
       happened, live, and on top of that the command reported success.

    The correct way is never to drop databases: `pg_dumpall
    --globals-only` for the roles (which a normal `pg_dump` loses) and one
    `pg_dump --clean --if-exists` per database, which deletes and recreates
    the OBJECTS inside without touching the container.
    """
    pod, ns = b["pod"], b["ns"]
    v = kubectl(["exec", "-n", ns, pod, "--", "postgres", "--version"])

    g = kubectl(["exec", "-n", ns, pod, "--", "sh", "-c",
                 'pg_dumpall -U "$POSTGRES_USER" --globals-only'], binary=True)
    if g.returncode != 0 or not g.stdout:
        die(f"pg_dumpall --globals-only failed over {ns}/{pod}: "
            f"{(g.stderr or b'').decode(errors='replace')[:200]}")
    fg = f"{b['org']}-{b['servicio']}-globals.sql"
    open(os.path.join(target, fg), "wb").write(g.stdout)

    databases = []
    for database in databases_of(ns, pod):
        d = kubectl(["exec", "-n", ns, pod, "--", "sh", "-c",
                     f'pg_dump -U "$POSTGRES_USER" --clean --if-exists '
                     f'-d {shq(database)}'],
                    binary=True)
        if d.returncode != 0:
            die(f"the pg_dump of {database} failed: "
                f"{(d.stderr or b'').decode(errors='replace')[:200]}")
        fn = f"{b['org']}-{b['servicio']}-{database}.sql"
        open(os.path.join(target, fn), "wb").write(d.stdout)
        databases.append({"nombre": database, "archivo": fn, "bytes": len(d.stdout),
                          "sha256": hashlib.sha256(d.stdout).hexdigest(),
                          "tablas": inventory(ns, pod, database)})

    # The keys of this dictionary are the bundle's MANIFEST, that is, the
    # on-disk format that `list` and `restore` read out of `.age` files
    # already written. They stay as they are: translating them would make
    # every existing bundle unreadable, which is a coordinated change and
    # not a rename.
    return {
        "tipo": "postgres", "org": b["org"], "servicio": b["servicio"], "ns": ns,
        "motor": (v.stdout or "").strip(),
        "globals": {"archivo": fg, "bytes": len(g.stdout),
                    "sha256": hashlib.sha256(g.stdout).hexdigest()},
        "bases": databases,
        "bytes": len(g.stdout) + sum(x["bytes"] for x in databases),
        "credencial_sha256": credential_fingerprint(ns, b["secreto"]),
        "secreto": b["secreto"],
    }


def capture_bucket(bk, target):
    ak, sk = s3_credential(bk["ns"])
    obj_dir = os.path.join(target, f"{bk['org']}-bucket")
    os.makedirs(obj_dir, exist_ok=True)
    objects = []
    with Tunnel("garage-system", "garage", 3900) as t:
        for listed in bucket_listing(t.host, bk["bucket"], ak, sk):
            key = listed["clave"]
            st, data, _ = s3("GET", t.host, f"/{bk['bucket']}/{key}", {}, ak, sk)
            if st != 200:
                die(f"downloading {key} gave HTTP {st}")
            # The key becomes a FLAT name: a key with slashes is a
            # directory tree the tar would have to reproduce, and the
            # restore reads the key from the manifest anyway.
            safe = key.replace("/", "%2F")
            open(os.path.join(obj_dir, safe), "wb").write(data)
            objects.append({"clave": key, "archivo": safe,
                            "bytes": len(data),
                            "sha256": hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest()})
    return {"tipo": "bucket", "org": bk["org"], "bucket": bk["bucket"],
            "directorio": f"{bk['org']}-bucket", "objetos": objects,
            "total_bytes": sum(o["bytes"] for o in objects)}


def _tmpl_run(cmd, database, stdin_data=None):
    """Runs one of the catalogue's dump/restore templates, with <database>
    filled in. The templates are full `kubectl exec` shell strings and
    read the credential from a file INSIDE the pod, so nothing secret
    ever reaches argv on either side — the same rule the rest of this
    file keeps. stdout comes back as bytes (a dump is an archive)."""
    full = cmd.replace("<database>", database)
    return subprocess.run(["bash", "-c", full], capture_output=True,
                          input=stdin_data)


def _mongosh(m, database, js):
    """Runs a mongosh --eval INSIDE the pod, with the credential read
    from the mounted file and NEVER on argv (on either side). The JS
    sees U, P and DB already bound; auth is `db.getSiblingDB("admin")
    .auth(U, P)` in the script itself, the same way the catalogue's
    `databases` template authenticates."""
    script = (
        "kubectl exec -n %s %s -- sh -c '\n"
        "  U=$(cat /etc/aegis/credential/usuario)\n"
        "  P=$(cat /etc/aegis/credential/password)\n"
        "  mongosh --quiet --eval \"var U=\\\"$U\\\",P=\\\"$P\\\",DB=\\\"%s\\\"\" --eval %s'\n"
        % (m["ns"], m["pod"], database, shq(js))
    )
    return subprocess.run(["bash", "-c", script], capture_output=True)


def mongo_databases(m):
    """The databases mongodb holds, minus admin/local/config — the list
    the catalogue's `databases` template prints."""
    r = _tmpl_run(m["tmpl"]["databases"], "")
    if r.returncode != 0:
        die(f"listing the databases of {m['ns']}/{m['servicio']} failed: "
            f"{(r.stderr or b'').decode(errors='replace')[:200]}")
    return [l for l in (r.stdout or b"").decode(errors="replace").splitlines() if l.strip()]


def mongo_inventory(m, database):
    """Collections and their document counts — mongodb's answer to the
    row inventory postgres gives, and what the restore is checked
    against. Auth travels in the script over stdin (a heredoc), never in
    argv, the same way the catalogue's `databases` template authenticates."""
    js = ('db.getSiblingDB("admin").auth(U,P);'
          'var d=db.getSiblingDB(DB);'
          'print(d.getCollectionNames().sort()'
          '.map(function(c){return c+" "+d.getCollection(c).countDocuments({})}).join("\\n"))')
    r = _mongosh(m, database, js)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return None
    cols = []
    for l in (r.stdout or b"").decode(errors="replace").splitlines():
        parts = l.rsplit(" ", 1)
        if len(parts) == 2 and parts[1].isdigit():
            cols.append({"coleccion": parts[0], "docs": int(parts[1])})
    return cols


def capture_mongo(m, target):
    """One archive per database (mongodump --archive --gzip), the mongo
    twin of capture_database. No globals: mongodb keeps its users in the
    admin database, and the demo's single root credential is the Secret
    the platform generated — which is what realigns on restore."""
    v = subprocess.run(["kubectl", "exec", "-n", m["ns"], m["pod"], "--",
                        "sh", "-c", "mongod --version 2>/dev/null | head -1"],
                       capture_output=True, text=True)
    bases = []
    for database in mongo_databases(m):
        d = _tmpl_run(m["tmpl"]["dump"], database)
        if d.returncode != 0 or not d.stdout:
            die(f"the mongodump of {database} failed: "
                f"{(d.stderr or b'').decode(errors='replace')[:200]}")
        fn = f"{m['org']}-{m['servicio']}-{database}.archive.gz"
        open(os.path.join(target, fn), "wb").write(d.stdout)
        bases.append({"nombre": database, "archivo": fn, "bytes": len(d.stdout),
                      "sha256": hashlib.sha256(d.stdout).hexdigest(),
                      "colecciones": mongo_inventory(m, database)})
    return {
        "tipo": m["tipo"], "org": m["org"], "servicio": m["servicio"], "ns": m["ns"],
        "motor": (v.stdout or "").strip(),
        "bases": bases,
        "bytes": sum(x["bytes"] for x in bases),
        "credencial_sha256": credential_fingerprint(m["ns"], m["secreto"]),
        "secreto": m["secreto"],
        "tmpl": m["tmpl"],
    }


def mongo_sizes(m):
    """(database, dataSize+indexSize) per database, asked of db.stats() —
    mongodb's pg_database_size. What does not answer goes out as -1, the
    same floor rule postgres follows."""
    out = []
    for database in mongo_databases(m):
        js = ('db.getSiblingDB("admin").auth(U,P);'
              'var s=db.getSiblingDB(DB).stats();'
              'print((s.dataSize||0)+(s.indexSize||0))')
        r = _mongosh(m, database, js)
        txt = (r.stdout or b"").decode(errors="replace").strip()
        out.append((database, int(txt) if r.returncode == 0 and txt.isdigit() else -1))
    return out



def bundle_age(p):
    return (time.time() - os.path.getmtime(p)) / 3600.0


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  THE OFF-SITE DESTINATION — the bundle leaves the machine
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Everything above this line produces a bundle and leaves it on a disk
# of the same house. The command has been saying so out loud at the end
# of every run —«the bundle stays on the SAME machine you want to
# survive»— and saying it is not fixing it: measured on 2026-08-29, the
# newest bundle of the live instance was three days old and had been
# made by hand.
#
# WHAT TRAVELS. The `.age` file AS IT IS, with a small sidecar. Never
# plaintext, and the guard for that is not a habit: `remote_push`
# refuses anything whose first bytes are not age's header, so the day
# somebody wires this to a `.tgz` the upload does not happen.
#
# WHAT THE SIDECAR CARRIES, and above all what it does NOT. It carries
# the sha256 and the size of the CIPHERTEXT, the organization and the
# capture stamp: exactly enough for the restore to know, before
# decrypting, that what came down is what went up. It carries nothing
# from INSIDE the bundle — the internal manifest knows table names and
# row counts, and that is the tenants' metadata. Uploading it beside the
# ciphertext would put in the clear, next to the safe, the list of what
# is in the safe.
#
# WHY R2 AND NOT A SECOND DISK. A second disk in the same house burns
# with the house. R2 is the operator's decision (its free tier does not
# charge for DOWNLOADING, and the download is the whole of a recovery),
# it speaks the same S3 the platform already signs for, and it lives in
# the account that already holds the zone and the tunnel — with the
# consequence §6 of the protocol states and this code cannot fix: if
# that account falls, the three fall together. Tolerable because the
# bundle is ciphertext; not invisible, because it has to be known.
REMOTE_ID_NAME = "r2_access_key_id"
REMOTE_KEY_NAME = "r2_secret_access_key"
# R2 ignores the region and the SIGNATURE does not: it goes into the
# credential scope, and `auto` is the value R2 documents. Signing with
# Garage's `garage` here gives SignatureDoesNotMatch, which reads
# exactly like a wrong key.
REMOTE_REGION = "auto"
# age's binary header. `age -o file` (no --armor, which is what capture
# does) always starts the file with this line.
AGE_MAGIC = b"age-encryption.org/v1"


# ── the encrypted store, from this side ─────────────────────────────
def store_path(name):
    return paths.secrets_dir() / f"{name}.enc"


def store_read(name):
    """One entry of the encrypted store, in bytes. None if it is NOT there.

    The three states of lib/secrets.sh's `restore_secret` are kept, and
    the third is the one that matters: an entry that EXISTS and does not
    decrypt is not the same as an absent one. Collapsing them is how a
    caller ends up regenerating material that is already synchronised
    with a third party — the bug that made that function grow an exit
    code of its own. Here the consequence would be adopting a second R2
    credential while the first one is still the one written into the
    bucket's token.
    """
    p = store_path(name)
    if not p.is_file():
        return None
    r = subprocess.run(["sops", "--decrypt", "--input-type", "binary",
                        "--output-type", "binary", str(p)], capture_output=True)
    if r.returncode != 0 or not r.stdout:
        die(f"{p.name} EXISTS in the store and does not decrypt. It is not "
            f"treated as absent, and nothing is regenerated: check that "
            f"SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE points at this instance's age key.\n"
            f"      sops: {(r.stderr or b'').decode(errors='replace')[:200]}")
    return r.stdout


def age_recipient():
    """The public half of the instance's age pair.

    Same order as lib/secrets.sh: the exported variable, the file the
    init leaves in the instance, and only then derived from the private
    key. A store entry encrypted to the WRONG recipient is unreadable by
    the very machine that wrote it, and it does not say so until the day
    it is read back.
    """
    pub = (os.environ.get("AGE_PUBLIC") or "").strip()
    if pub:
        return pub
    marked = paths.aegis_home() / ".age-public"
    if marked.is_file() and marked.read_text().strip():
        return marked.read_text().strip()
    key = os.environ.get("SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE",
                         os.path.expanduser("~/.config/sops/age/aegis.key"))
    if os.path.exists(key):
        r = subprocess.run(["age-keygen", "-y", key], capture_output=True, text=True)
        if r.returncode == 0 and r.stdout.strip():
            return r.stdout.strip()
    die("no age recipient: neither AGE_PUBLIC, nor the instance's "
        ".age-public, nor a private key to derive it from. Without it "
        "nothing can be written to the store")


def store_write(name, data):
    """Writes one entry to the encrypted store, with the same discipline.

    Three properties copied from `persist_secret`, and each one has a
    corpse behind it: the redirection goes to a `.tmp` (a failure
    halfway used to leave the previous ciphertext at 0 bytes, losing
    material already registered with a third party), the ROUNDTRIP is
    verified over that `.tmp` before it counts, and only then does an
    atomic `mv` replace what was there.
    """
    d = paths.secrets_dir()
    d.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    os.chmod(d, 0o700)
    dest = store_path(name)
    tmp = dest.with_suffix(".enc.tmp")
    plain = os.path.join(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="aegis-store-", dir="/dev/shm"
                                          if os.path.isdir("/dev/shm") else None),
                         name)
    try:
        with open(os.open(plain, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_TRUNC, 0o600), "wb") as f:
            f.write(data)
        r = subprocess.run(["sops", "--encrypt", "--age", age_recipient(),
                            "--input-type", "binary", "--output-type", "binary",
                            plain], capture_output=True)
        if r.returncode != 0 or not r.stdout:
            die(f"the store could not encrypt {name} — what was already there "
                f"is left INTACT: {(r.stderr or b'').decode(errors='replace')[:200]}")
        tmp.write_bytes(r.stdout)
        os.chmod(tmp, 0o600)
        back = subprocess.run(["sops", "--decrypt", "--input-type", "binary",
                               "--output-type", "binary", str(tmp)],
                              capture_output=True)
        if back.returncode != 0 or back.stdout != data:
            tmp.unlink(missing_ok=True)
            die(f"the store's roundtrip FAILED for {name}: what was written "
                f"does not read back as what was handed in. Nothing was replaced")
        tmp.replace(dest)
        os.chmod(dest, 0o600)
    finally:
        if os.path.exists(plain):
            subprocess.run(["shred", "-u", plain], capture_output=True)
        shutil.rmtree(os.path.dirname(plain), ignore_errors=True)


# ── where the destination is, derived and never written twice ───────
def remote_bucket_name(root_domain):
    """The destination bucket, derived from the instance's root domain.

    ONE derivation, in ONE place, because this name is written in three:
    the tofu that creates the bucket, the access policy of the token
    that may write into it, and the PUT this command makes. Two of the
    three agreeing and the third not is a backup that reports success
    against a bucket nobody made — R2 answers 404 and the run says the
    sink failed, which is the good case; the bad one is a name that
    exists and belongs to something else.

    R2 accepts lowercase, digits and hyphens, 3 to 63 characters. A
    domain is already almost that: the dots become hyphens.
    """
    slug = re.sub(r"[^a-z0-9-]+", "-", (root_domain or "").strip().lower()).strip("-")
    if not slug:
        die("the instance's aegis.conf declares no ROOT_DOMAIN, so the "
            "destination bucket cannot be derived. On an instance with no "
            "domain there is no Cloudflare account either, and the off-site "
            "copy has no subject")
    return f"aegis-backups-{slug}"[:63].rstrip("-")


def remote_config():
    """Account, bucket and endpoint — out of the instance's own config.

    Nothing new is asked of the operator: the account is the one the
    wizard already captured (`CF_ACCOUNT_ID`) and the bucket comes out
    of the root domain. The endpoint's shape
    (`<account>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com`, path-style, region `auto`)
    was verified on 2026-08-29 against developers.cloudflare.com/r2.
    """
    conf = paths.read_conf()
    account = (conf.get("CF_ACCOUNT_ID") or "").strip()
    if not account:
        die("the instance's aegis.conf names no Cloudflare account: there "
            "is no R2 to send the bundle to. With a local edge that is the "
            "correct state, and the protocol says what it costs")
    return {"account": account,
            "bucket": remote_bucket_name(conf.get("ROOT_DOMAIN")),
            "host": f"{account}.r2.cloudflarestorage.com"}


def remote_credential():
    """The destination's S3 pair, out of the encrypted store.

    It is NOT read from the environment and NOT taken on the command
    line: a key in argv is world-readable in /proc, and a key in the
    environment survives into every child process. The store is the same
    one the rest of the instance's material lives in, opened with the
    same age key.
    """
    ak = store_read(REMOTE_ID_NAME)
    sk = store_read(REMOTE_KEY_NAME)
    if ak is None or sk is None:
        raise cli.CouldNotEvaluate(
            f"this instance has adopted no off-site destination: "
            f"{REMOTE_ID_NAME}/{REMOTE_KEY_NAME} are not in the encrypted "
            f"store. Adopt one with `{cli.cmd('data')} remote adopt`")
    return ak.decode().strip(), sk.decode().strip()


def remote_s3(method, key, query=None, body=b"", timeout=30, cfg=None, pair=None):
    """One request against the off-site destination, through the ONE signer."""
    cfg = cfg or remote_config()
    ak, sk = pair or remote_credential()
    path = f"/{cfg['bucket']}" + (f"/{key}" if key else "")
    return s3(method, cfg["host"], path, query or {}, ak, sk,
              region=REMOTE_REGION, body=body, scheme="https", timeout=timeout)


# ── what goes up ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def remote_sidecar(bundle, org):
    """The fingerprint of the CIPHERTEXT, and nothing else.

    What the restore needs before touching anything is one question:
    is what came down byte for byte what went up? The sha256 of the
    `.age` answers it and costs nothing to carry.

    What is deliberately NOT here is everything the bundle's internal
    manifest knows — database names, table names, row counts. That is
    the tenants' metadata, and putting it in the clear beside the
    ciphertext would publish the index of the safe next to the safe.
    """
    data = open(bundle, "rb").read()
    return {
        "version": 1,
        "archivo": os.path.basename(bundle),
        "organizacion": org,
        "bytes": len(data),
        "sha256": hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest(),
        "subido": datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc)
                  .strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ"),
    }


def remote_key(org, bundle):
    return f"org-{org}/{os.path.basename(bundle)}"


def remote_push(bundle, org):
    """Uploads one bundle and its sidecar, and READS BACK what it wrote.

    THE GUARD FIRST, and it is not ceremony. This function is the one
    door out of the machine, and the day somebody wires it to a
    directory of `.tgz` —or to the staging file the capture builds
    before encrypting— the tenants' data would leave in the clear over
    somebody else's wire, and nothing would say so: the upload would
    succeed. So the check is on the CONTENT and not on the name: age's
    own header, read from the first bytes of the file.

    And what was written is read back with a HEAD, because an S3 PUT
    that returns 200 is a promise and the listing is the measurement —
    the same rule the restore of the bucket follows a few hundred lines
    above.
    """
    with open(bundle, "rb") as f:
        head = f.read(len(AGE_MAGIC))
    if head != AGE_MAGIC:
        die(f"{os.path.basename(bundle)} is NOT an age-encrypted file (its "
            f"first bytes are not age's header) and NOTHING was uploaded. "
            f"Only ciphertext leaves this machine: what goes to a third "
            f"party's disk is protected by the instance's key or it does "
            f"not go")
    cfg = remote_config()
    pair = remote_credential()
    data = open(bundle, "rb").read()
    key = remote_key(org, bundle)
    st, body, _ = remote_s3("PUT", key, body=data, timeout=600, cfg=cfg, pair=pair)
    if st not in (200, 201):
        die(f"the bundle did NOT leave the machine: PUT {key} gave HTTP {st} "
            f"({body[:160]!r}). The copy is on this disk and only on this disk")
    st, _, headers = remote_s3("HEAD", key, cfg=cfg, pair=pair)
    got = int(headers.get("Content-Length", -1)) if st == 200 else -1
    if got != len(data):
        die(f"{key}: uploaded, and the destination returns {got} bytes where "
            f"{len(data)} were expected — it is not called good")
    side = json.dumps(remote_sidecar(bundle, org), indent=2,
                      ensure_ascii=False).encode()
    st, body, _ = remote_s3("PUT", key + ".manifiesto.json", body=side,
                            cfg=cfg, pair=pair)
    if st not in (200, 201):
        die(f"the bundle went up and its manifest did NOT ({st}): a copy "
            f"whose integrity cannot be checked before restoring is a copy "
            f"nobody can trust on the day it is needed")
    ok(f"off-site: {cfg['bucket']}/{key} ({len(data)} bytes) and its manifest")
    return key


# ── what comes down ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
def remote_listing(cfg=None, pair=None):
    """Every object at the destination, through the ONE lister.

    `bucket_listing` already knows how to page ListObjectsV2, which is
    the detail that is written well once and badly the other two times.
    R2 answers the same protocol, so it is the same function.
    """
    cfg = cfg or remote_config()
    ak, sk = pair or remote_credential()
    return bucket_listing(cfg["host"], cfg["bucket"], ak, sk,
                          scheme="https", region=REMOTE_REGION)


def remote_fetch(key, target):
    """Brings one bundle down and CHECKS IT before anything else exists.

    The order is the whole point, and it is the same one the restore of
    a bucket follows: the sidecar comes first, the object second, and
    the sha256 is compared BEFORE the file is handed to anybody. A
    truncated download is not a rare event over a home connection, and a
    truncated `.age` does not fail loudly — `age -d` says the file is
    corrupt, which reads like «the key is wrong» on the worst possible
    day.
    """
    cfg = remote_config()
    pair = remote_credential()
    st, side, _ = remote_s3("GET", key + ".manifiesto.json", cfg=cfg, pair=pair)
    if st != 200:
        die(f"{key} has no manifest at the destination (HTTP {st}): what came "
            f"down cannot be checked against anything, and a bundle that "
            f"cannot be checked is not restored")
    try:
        man = json.loads(side)
    except ValueError:
        die(f"the manifest of {key} is not readable JSON: nothing is restored")
    st, data, headers = remote_s3("GET", key, timeout=600, cfg=cfg, pair=pair)
    if st != 200:
        die(f"downloading {key} gave HTTP {st}")
    got = hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest()
    if len(data) != man.get("bytes") or got != man.get("sha256"):
        die(f"{key} does NOT reproduce its manifest ({len(data)} bytes, "
            f"sha256 {got[:16]}…; {man.get('bytes')} and "
            f"{(man.get('sha256') or '?')[:16]}… were expected). NOTHING was "
            f"written: the transfer was cut, or the object at the destination "
            f"is not the one that was uploaded")
    with open(os.open(target, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_TRUNC, 0o600), "wb") as f:
        f.write(data)
    ok(f"{key} downloaded and VERIFIED against its manifest "
       f"({len(data)} bytes, sha256 {got[:16]}…)")
    return man


def capture(root, remote=True):
    """One bundle per organization, each one in its folder.

    The walk comes out of `sources()`, which already crosses the contracts
    with the cluster and refuses to back up if they disagree. Here it is
    only grouped: one organization = one folder = one bundle.
    """
    root = prepare_root(root)
    print(f"{gray}sources (contracts ∩ cluster):{reset}")
    databases, mongos, buckets = sources()
    for b in databases:
        print(f"  {gray}·{reset} postgres  {b['ns']}/{b['servicio']}")
    for m in mongos:
        print(f"  {gray}·{reset} {m['tipo']:8}  {m['ns']}/{m['servicio']}")
    for b in buckets:
        print(f"  {gray}·{reset} bucket    {b['bucket']}  {gray}(shared garage){reset}")
    if not databases and not mongos and not buckets:
        die("no contract declares data to back up")

    per_org = {}
    for b in databases:
        per_org.setdefault(b["org"], ([], [], []))[0].append(b)
    for m in mongos:
        per_org.setdefault(m["org"], ([], [], []))[1].append(m)
    for bk in buckets:
        per_org.setdefault(bk["org"], ([], [], []))[2].append(bk)

    stamp = time.strftime("%Y%m%d-%H%M%S")
    rc = 0
    done_orgs = []
    for org in sorted(per_org):
        ob, omongo, obk = per_org[org]
        folder = os.path.join(root, f"org-{org}")
        os.makedirs(folder, exist_ok=True)
        # The name repeats the organization even though the folder already
        # says it: a bundle that travels alone —copied to another disk,
        # attached to a ticket— has to be able to say what it is without
        # its context.
        #
        # And the name is a CONTRACT with aegis check, which looks for
        # exactly this pattern to tell whether an organization has a
        # backup: it is not translated.
        out = os.path.join(folder, f"aegis-datos-org-{org}-{stamp}.age")
        print(f"\n{gray}── organization {org}{reset}")
        r = capture_org(org, ob, omongo, obk, out, remote=remote)
        rc |= r
        if r == 0:
            done_orgs.append(org)
    write_readme(root, sorted(per_org), done_orgs, stamp)
    return rc


def write_readme(root, expected, done_orgs, stamp):
    """The map of the backups tree, for whoever opens it a year from now.

    It lives in the ROOT of the destination and not in the repo, because
    the moment it is needed is precisely the one in which there is no
    repo: a disk plugged into another machine after losing this one.
    Safekeeping you do not know how to read is half safekeeping.

    And it is REWRITTEN on every capture instead of being written once,
    for the usual reason: a file drafted by hand ages and ends up
    describing a tree that no longer exists — with the particularity that
    here nobody was ever going to contrast it, because this directory is
    not versioned and no git check looks at it. Derived, it cannot lie for
    longer than it takes to get to the next run.

    It enumerates the organizations the CONTRACTS declare, not the folders
    that are there: a missing folder is exactly the finding, and listing
    the directory would hide it.

    The file is called LEER.md and stays that way: it is the on-disk name
    on every backup disk already in use, and renaming it would leave a
    stale twin beside the new one.
    """
    missing = [o for o in expected if o not in done_orgs]
    lines = [
        "# aegis backups — how this is put together",
        "",
        f"Last capture: {stamp} (the stamp of that run's bundles).",
        "THIS FILE IS WRITTEN BY `aegis data backup`. Do not edit it:",
        "the next capture overwrites it.",
        "",
        "## What is in each folder",
        "",
        "```",
        ".aegis-destino    the disk's mark. If its UUID does not match the",
        "                  filesystem this folder is on, the backups disk is",
        "                  NOT mounted and the capture refuses to write —",
        "                  without that the bundle would fall on the same",
        "                  disk it has to survive.",
        "platform/         the STATE: the store's secrets, the init's trail,",
        "                  the edge's tfstate. Written by aegis state backup.",
        "org-<name>/       the DATA of ONE organization: its databases and",
        "                  its objects. One bundle per organization, never",
        "                  shared.",
        "```",
        "",
        "**Both halves are needed.** With the state you raise an empty",
        "platform; with the data you have a dump nobody knows where to put.",
        "",
        "## The organizations, according to the contracts",
        "",
    ]
    for o in expected:
        if o in done_orgs:
            lines.append(f"- `org-{o}/` — backed up in this run.")
        else:
            lines.append(f"- `org-{o}/` — **WITHOUT a backup in this run**: its "
                         "capture failed. Whatever is in its folder is older.")
    if missing:
        lines += ["",
                  "> An organization that appears here and has no folder is one that",
                  "> was NEVER backed up. `aegis check` enumerates it against the",
                  "> contracts precisely so that that absence has a shape."]
    lines += [
        "",
        "## The encryption, and the only way to lose all of this",
        "",
        "Every `.age` is encrypted with the PUBLIC key of aegis's pair and opens",
        "with the private one: `~/.config/sops/age/aegis.key`, the same one SOPS",
        "uses for the repo's `.enc` files. One single pair for both things.",
        "",
        "**That key is NOT in any bundle**, on purpose and with a guard that",
        "aborts the capture if it appears inside. The chicken and the egg in the",
        "same basket protect nothing. What follows from that has to be said:",
        "",
        "> **If you lost the age key, everything on this disk is a brick.**",
        "> There is no recovery, neither partial nor by brute force.",
        "> The key goes to offline safekeeping, and that has to be a place",
        "> DIFFERENT from this disk — if they live together, one accident takes",
        "> both halves.",
        "",
        "The encryption is not ceremony: the `platform/` bundle carries the",
        "tunnel's tfstate with its token IN THE CLEAR. The bundle's `age` is the",
        "only thing protecting it.",
        "",
        "## How one is opened",
        "",
        "Without anything of aegis, which is the case this file exists for —",
        "this disk plugged into another machine, without a repo. `age` and",
        "`tar` are enough, and out comes SQL and plain files:",
        "",
        "```",
        "age -d -i ~/.config/sops/age/aegis.key <bundle>.age | tar -xzvf -",
        "```",
        "",
        "With the repo at hand there is convenience —it validates the manifest,",
        "compares credentials, restores against the right database—, but it is",
        "not a proprietary format:",
        "",
        "```",
        "aegis data list    org-<org>/aegis-datos-org-<org>-<ts>.age",
        "aegis data restore org-<org>/aegis-datos-org-<org>-<ts>.age --org <org>",
        "```",
        "",
        "`list` opens the bundle and says what it brings without restoring anything.",
        "",
        "Every bundle was verified as it was written: the tool decrypts it right",
        "after creating it and compares it byte by byte with what it captured. If",
        "one says NOT-VERIFIED, it was produced without the private key at hand.",
        "",
        "## legado/",
        "",
        "If it exists, those are bundles of the old format (before 2026-08-11)",
        "with ALL the organizations inside a single file. Its own LEER.md",
        "explains it. They are not moved into an `org-*` folder because they do",
        "not belong to one organization: counting them as one organization's",
        "backup would be a lie.",
        "",
    ]
    with open(os.path.join(root, "LEER.md"), "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        f.write("\n".join(lines))
    ok(f"LEER.md up to date in {root} ({len(expected)} organization(s) according to the contracts)")


def capture_org(org, databases, mongos, buckets, out, remote=True):
    stage = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="aegis-resp-")
    pay = os.path.join(stage, "payload"); os.makedirs(pay)
    try:
        pieces = []
        for b in databases:
            m = capture_database(b, pay); pieces.append(m)
            step(f"postgres {m['org']}/{m['servicio']}: {m['bytes']} bytes "
                 f"({m['motor']})")
        for mo in mongos:
            m = capture_mongo(mo, pay); pieces.append(m)
            step(f"{m['tipo']} {m['org']}/{m['servicio']}: {m['bytes']} bytes, "
                 f"{len(m['bases'])} database(s)")
        for bk in buckets:
            m = capture_bucket(bk, pay); pieces.append(m)
            step(f"bucket {m['bucket']}: {len(m['objetos'])} objects, "
                 f"{m['total_bytes']} bytes")

        man = {
            "version": 2,          # v2: one bundle = one organization
            "organizacion": org,
            "capturado": datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc)
                          .strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ"),
            "instancia": subprocess.run(
                ["kubectl", "get", "ns", "kube-system", "-o", "jsonpath={.metadata.uid}"],
                capture_output=True, text=True).stdout.strip(),
            "piezas": pieces,
        }
        with open(os.path.join(pay, "MANIFIESTO.json"), "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            json.dump(man, f, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False)

        # HARD GUARD, the same one as aegis state backup: the age key never
        # gets into the bundle. The chicken and the egg do not travel
        # together.
        for r_, _, fs in os.walk(pay):
            for x in fs:
                if x in ("aegis.key", "keys.txt") or x.endswith(".agekey"):
                    die("the payload contains age key material — ABORTED")

        tgz = os.path.join(stage, "bundle.tgz")
        with tarfile.open(tgz, "w:gz") as tf:
            tf.add(pay, arcname=".")

        pub = os.environ.get("AGE_PUBLIC") or ""
        if not pub:
            age_key = os.environ.get("SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE",
                                     os.path.expanduser("~/.config/sops/age/aegis.key"))
            if not os.path.exists(age_key):
                die("neither AGE_PUBLIC nor an age key to derive the recipient from")
            pub = subprocess.run(["age-keygen", "-y", age_key],
                                 capture_output=True, text=True).stdout.strip()
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(out) or ".", exist_ok=True)
        r = subprocess.run(["age", "-r", pub, "-o", out, tgz],
                           capture_output=True, text=True)
        if r.returncode != 0:
            die(f"age failed: {r.stderr.strip()[:200]}")
        os.chmod(out, 0o600)
        ok(f"age-encrypted bundle → {out} ({os.path.getsize(out)} bytes)")

        # ── ROUNDTRIP. Without this it is "we have backups", not a proven
        # restore. It is really decrypted and compared byte by byte.
        age_key = os.environ.get("SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE",
                                 os.path.expanduser("~/.config/sops/age/aegis.key"))
        if not os.path.exists(age_key):
            print(f"  {yellow}!{reset} without the private age key: the bundle exists but was "
                  f"NOT verified (DR without proof of restore)")
            return 0
        check_dir = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="aegis-ver-")
        try:
            d = subprocess.run(["age", "-d", "-i", age_key, out], capture_output=True)
            if d.returncode != 0:
                die("the bundle does not decrypt with the age key — do NOT trust it")
            with tarfile.open(fileobj=__import__("io").BytesIO(d.stdout), mode="r:gz") as tf:
                tf.extractall(check_dir, filter="data")
            bad_ones = []
            for p in pieces:
                if p["tipo"] == "postgres":
                    for piece in [p["globals"]] + p["bases"]:
                        q = os.path.join(check_dir, piece["archivo"])
                        if not os.path.exists(q) or \
                           hashlib.sha256(open(q, "rb").read()).hexdigest() != piece["sha256"]:
                            bad_ones.append(f"{p['org']}/{p['servicio']}:{piece['archivo']}")
                elif p["tipo"] == "mongodb":
                    for piece in p["bases"]:
                        q = os.path.join(check_dir, piece["archivo"])
                        if not os.path.exists(q) or \
                           hashlib.sha256(open(q, "rb").read()).hexdigest() != piece["sha256"]:
                            bad_ones.append(f"{p['org']}/{p['servicio']}:{piece['archivo']}")
                else:
                    for o in p["objetos"]:
                        q = os.path.join(check_dir, p["directorio"], o["archivo"])
                        if not os.path.exists(q) or \
                           hashlib.sha256(open(q, "rb").read()).hexdigest() != o["sha256"]:
                            bad_ones.append(f"{p['bucket']}:{o['clave']}")
            if bad_ones:
                die(f"ROUNDTRIP FAILED — it does not reproduce: {', '.join(bad_ones)}")
            ok(f"ROUNDTRIP OK — the bundle decrypts and reproduces the "
               f"{len(pieces)} pieces byte by byte")
        finally:
            shutil.rmtree(check_dir, ignore_errors=True)
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(stage, ignore_errors=True)

    # ── AND NOW IT LEAVES THE HOUSE ─────────────────────────────
    #
    # Here, and not before: what goes out is a bundle that has already
    # been decrypted and compared byte by byte against what was
    # captured. Uploading an unverified bundle would be filling a third
    # party's disk with something nobody has proved is a backup.
    #
    # The order between the two exits is not arbitrary either. The
    # off-site destination is the one that answers the question this
    # whole section exists for, so it goes first and its failure is
    # fatal: a run that reports success having left the copy on the
    # machine it has to survive is the lie the local disk already told
    # for months. `AEGIS_BACKUP_SINK` stays as it was, for whoever wired
    # their own transport before this existed.
    left = []
    if remote:
        try:
            remote_push(out, org)
            left.append("off-site")
        except cli.CouldNotEvaluate as e:
            print(f"  {yellow}!{reset} {e}")
    sink = os.environ.get("AEGIS_BACKUP_SINK")
    if sink:
        r = subprocess.run(["bash", "-c", sink, "aegis-sink", out])
        if r.returncode != 0:
            die(f"the sink FAILED — the bundle is at {out} but it did not leave here")
        ok("sink OK — the bundle left the machine")
        left.append("sink")
    if not left:
        print(f"  {yellow}!{reset} the bundle did NOT leave the machine: no adopted "
              f"off-site destination and no AEGIS_BACKUP_SINK. It stays on the "
              f"SAME machine you want to survive")
    return 0


# ── reading and restoring ───────────────────────────────────────────
def open_bundle(bundle):
    age_key = os.environ.get("SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE",
                             os.path.expanduser("~/.config/sops/age/aegis.key"))
    if not os.path.exists(age_key):
        die(f"without the private age key ({age_key}) — it is the irreducible; without "
            f"it the bundle does not decrypt, and so it must be")
    d = subprocess.run(["age", "-d", "-i", age_key, bundle], capture_output=True)
    if d.returncode != 0:
        die(f"it does not decrypt: {d.stderr.decode(errors='replace')[:200]}")
    tmp = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="aegis-rest-")
    with tarfile.open(fileobj=__import__("io").BytesIO(d.stdout), mode="r:gz") as tf:
        tf.extractall(tmp, filter="data")
    man = json.load(open(os.path.join(tmp, "MANIFIESTO.json"), encoding="utf-8"))
    return tmp, man


def list_bundle(bundle):
    tmp, man = open_bundle(bundle)
    try:
        print(f"{gray}captured:{reset} {man['capturado']}   "
              f"{gray}instance:{reset} {man['instancia'][:12]}…")
        print(f"{gray}age:{reset} {bundle_age(bundle):.1f} h\n")
        for p in man["piezas"]:
            if p["tipo"] == "postgres":
                print(f"  postgres  {p['org']}/{p['servicio']:10} {p['bytes']:>9} bytes  "
                      f"{gray}{p['motor']}{reset}")
                for b_ in p["bases"]:
                    tt = b_["tablas"] or []
                    print(f"            {gray}database {b_['nombre']}: {b_['bytes']} B, "
                          f"{len(tt)} tables, {sum(x['filas'] for x in tt)} rows{reset}")
                print(f"            {gray}expected credential sha256:"
                      f"{(p.get('credencial_sha256') or '?')[:16]}…{reset}")
            elif p["tipo"] == "mongodb":
                print(f"  mongodb   {p['org']}/{p['servicio']:10} {p['bytes']:>9} bytes  "
                      f"{gray}{p['motor']}{reset}")
                for b_ in p["bases"]:
                    cc = b_.get("colecciones") or []
                    print(f"            {gray}database {b_['nombre']}: {b_['bytes']} B, "
                          f"{len(cc)} collections, {sum(x['docs'] for x in cc)} docs{reset}")
                print(f"            {gray}expected credential sha256:"
                      f"{(p.get('credencial_sha256') or '?')[:16]}…{reset}")
            else:
                print(f"  bucket    {p['bucket']:20} {len(p['objetos'])} objects, "
                      f"{p['total_bytes']} bytes")
                for o in p["objetos"][:5]:
                    print(f"            {gray}{o['clave']}  {o['bytes']} B{reset}")
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(tmp, ignore_errors=True)
    return 0


def scale_consumers_down(ns):
    """Scales to zero whatever may have the database open, and remembers to what.

    ArgoCD has selfHeal on these apps, so it is going to want to give the
    replicas back. The window is seconds and the restore is faster, but if
    one day it stops being so the symptom would be a DROP DATABASE that
    fails, not corrupted data — which is the correct way to fail.
    """
    r = kubectl(["get", "deploy", "-n", ns, "-o",
                 "jsonpath={range .items[*]}{.metadata.name} {.spec.replicas}{\"\\n\"}{end}"])
    previous = {}
    for l in (r.stdout or "").splitlines():
        if not l.strip():
            continue
        n, rep = l.split()
        previous[n] = int(rep)
    for n, rep in previous.items():
        if rep:
            kubectl(["scale", "deploy", n, "-n", ns, "--replicas=0"])
    if previous:
        step(f"consumers down: {', '.join(f'{n}({r})' for n, r in previous.items())}")
        for _ in range(30):
            q = kubectl(["get", "pods", "-n", ns, "-l", "!aegis.dev/component",
                         "--no-headers"])
            if not (q.stdout or "").strip():
                break
            time.sleep(2)
    return previous


def scale_consumers_up(ns, previous):
    for n, rep in previous.items():
        if rep:
            kubectl(["scale", "deploy", n, "-n", ns, f"--replicas={rep}"])
    if previous:
        step(f"consumers back up: "
             f"{', '.join(f'{n}({r})' for n, r in previous.items())}")


def cut_connections(ns, pod):
    """The sessions left over after bringing the pods down.

    A pod that is going away can leave the connection for a while, and ONE
    is enough for DROP DATABASE to fail.
    """
    kubectl(["exec", "-n", ns, pod, "--", "sh", "-c",
             'psql -U "$POSTGRES_USER" -d postgres -Atc '
             '"SELECT pg_terminate_backend(pid) FROM pg_stat_activity '
             'WHERE pid <> pg_backend_pid() AND datname IS NOT NULL;"'])


def role_can_log_in(ns, pod, user, password, database):
    """Opens the database WITH the Secret's credential, the way the app does.

    Over the unix socket this would prove NOTHING: the image's pg_hba
    trusts local connections, so a wrong password gets in all the same and
    the test would be green by construction. 127.0.0.1 goes through the
    `host` rules, which are the ones the application meets — the same
    principle as everywhere else here: the defining test is the one the
    real consumer makes.

    The password reaches the container over stdin and is read by the shell
    that is already inside. It never appears in argv, not on the host's
    side and not in the pod's, which is where `/proc/PID/cmdline` is
    world-readable.
    """
    r = kubectl(["exec", "-i", "-n", ns, pod, "--", "sh", "-c",
                 "IFS= read -r PGPASSWORD; export PGPASSWORD; "
                 f"exec psql -h 127.0.0.1 -U {shq(user)} -d {shq(database)} "
                 f"-Atc {shq('SELECT 1')}"],
                binary=True, stdin_data=(password + "\n").encode())
    return r.returncode == 0 and (r.stdout or b"").strip() == b"1"


def realign_role(ns, pod, secret, database):
    """Puts the role's password back in step with the Secret of THIS instance.

    The globals dump carries the SCRAM hash each role had AT CAPTURE
    TIME, so loading it reinstalls the OLD password. Restoring on the
    machine that took the capture, that is exactly what you want.
    Restoring on a NEW one it is not: that instance generated its own
    credential, with its own key, and the dump knows nothing about it —
    the database ends up expecting one password and the pod presenting
    another. The symptom is an `authentication failed` in the
    application, which looks like a bug in the application.

    Measured in the rehearsal of 2026-08-27: the restore finished, said
    it was verified, and the organization did not come up until somebody
    typed an ALTER ROLE by hand. A step that is documented and not
    executed is a step that does not exist.

    The password never becomes an argument: the statement travels as
    psql's script, over stdin, the same road the dump takes.
    """
    user = secret_value(ns, secret, "usuario")
    password = secret_value(ns, secret, "password")
    if user is None or password is None:
        die(f"{ns}/{secret} does not carry `usuario` and `password`: the role "
            f"cannot be realigned, and a restore without realigning leaves the "
            f"application locked out of its own database")
    # STRICT, and it dies instead of repairing. `errors="replace"` turns
    # each invalid byte into U+FFFD in silence, and what would be
    # installed by the ALTER ROLE is THAT mutilated string — while the pod
    # keeps receiving the raw bytes in its environment variable. The proof
    # underneath would not catch it either: it would compare the mutilated
    # password against itself and come out green. The same shape as the
    # line break below, and the same conclusion: the credential is already
    # broken before this, and repairing it in silence only moves the
    # failure to the application.
    try:
        user = user.decode().strip()
        password = password.decode()
    except UnicodeDecodeError:
        die(f"the credential of {ns}/{secret} is not valid UTF-8: it cannot "
            f"become a SQL statement without mutilating it, and a mutilated "
            f"password would be installed here and then verified against "
            f"itself — green, with the application locked out. Regenerate "
            f"the Secret")
    if any(c in password for c in "\r\n\x00"):
        die(f"the password in {ns}/{secret} carries a line break: it cannot "
            f"travel over a line-oriented channel — and the container's "
            f"environment variable would not carry it either, so the "
            f"credential is already broken before this")

    sql = (f"ALTER ROLE {sql_identifier(user)} WITH LOGIN "
           f"PASSWORD {sql_literal(password)};\n")
    r = kubectl(["exec", "-i", "-n", ns, pod, "--", "sh", "-c",
                 'psql -U "$POSTGRES_USER" -d postgres -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1'],
                binary=True, stdin_data=sql.encode())
    if r.returncode != 0:
        # Its output is NOT reproduced, and that is not laziness: on a
        # syntax error psql echoes the offending LINE, and that line is
        # the one carrying the credential.
        die(f"the ALTER ROLE over {ns}/{pod} failed (psql rc {r.returncode}). "
            f"Its output is not shown: the statement that failed carries the "
            f"credential")
    if not role_can_log_in(ns, pod, user, password, database):
        die(f"the ALTER ROLE reported no error and the credential of "
            f"{ns}/{secret} still does not open {database}. That the command "
            f"did not complain is not proof: the drill of 2026-08-09 reported "
            f"success over a database that had been deleted")
    return user


def restore_bucket(p, payload):
    """Puts the bundle's objects back into the destination's bucket.

    Until 2026-08-27 this half printed «not implemented» and stopped. The
    database came back and the bucket did not, which is the worst of the
    three possible states: the rows that came back point at objects that
    are no longer there, and nothing says so out loud — the application
    serves 404s over data the operator believes restored.

    Three decisions, with their reasons:

    · THE CREDENTIAL IS THE DESTINATION'S. The bundle carries objects,
      never keys, so the pair comes out of the live `garage` Secret of
      the organization's namespace. The namespace is not in the manifest
      either (the bucket piece never carried one): it is derived the way
      `sources()` derives it — one organization, one `org-<name>`.

    · AN OBJECT THAT IS ALREADY THERE IS OVERWRITTEN, unless it is
      IDENTICAL. Restoring means bringing the organization back to the
      captured state, and the database half already overwrites
      (`pg_dump --clean`); skipping the objects would leave the two
      halves at different dates, which is the very inconsistency this
      command exists to avoid. Identical is skipped by CONTENT and not
      by name —the ETag of a single-part upload is the md5 of the body—
      so re-running a restore is cheap and, above all, what the report
      says it wrote is what actually changed.

    · WHAT IS ONLY IN THE DESTINATION IS NOT DELETED, it is named. An
      object the bucket has and the bundle does not is later than the
      capture; deleting it would turn a restore into a loss no bundle
      can undo. Naming it is enough for whoever is restoring to decide.
    """
    ns = f"org-{p['org']}"
    bucket = p["bucket"]

    # THE WHOLE PAYLOAD AGAINST THE MANIFEST, AND ONLY THEN THE FIRST PUT.
    # This is the order the database half has always followed, and until
    # the review of 2026-08-29 the comment here claimed it while the code
    # did the other thing: it hashed each object INSIDE the write loop, so
    # a bad sha256 on object N died with objects 1..N-1 already in the
    # bucket. Half a restore is the state this command exists to avoid,
    # and a comment that lies about the order is worse than no comment,
    # because the next reader believes it.
    #
    # Every mismatch is named at once, and for the same reason the
    # verification at the end names them all: what to do with a bundle
    # that does not reproduce itself is a person's decision, and they
    # decide better seeing the whole list than its first item.
    broken = []
    for o in p["objetos"]:
        src = os.path.join(payload, p["directorio"], o["archivo"])
        if not os.path.exists(src):
            broken.append(f"{o['clave']}: it is not in the bundle's payload")
        elif hashlib.sha256(open(src, "rb").read()).hexdigest() != o["sha256"]:
            broken.append(f"{o['clave']}: {o['archivo']} does not match its "
                          f"sha256 from the manifest")
    if broken:
        for x in broken[:12]:
            print(f"  {red}✗{reset} {x}")
        die(f"{p['org']}/{bucket}: the bundle does not reproduce itself — "
            f"NOTHING was written")

    ak, sk = s3_credential(ns)
    written = identical = 0
    problems = []
    with Tunnel("garage-system", "garage", 3900) as t:
        for o in p["objetos"]:
            data = open(os.path.join(payload, p["directorio"],
                                     o["archivo"]), "rb").read()
            # md5 for the ETag, which is an S3 identifier and not a
            # security claim — hence usedforsecurity=False, so it also
            # works where the library forbids md5 for hashing.
            etag = hashlib.md5(data, usedforsecurity=False).hexdigest()
            st, _, headers = s3("HEAD", t.host, f"/{bucket}/{o['clave']}", {}, ak, sk)
            if st == 200 and (headers.get("ETag") or "").strip('"') == etag:
                identical += 1
                step(f"object {o['clave']}: already identical ({len(data)} bytes)")
                continue
            st, body, _ = s3("PUT", t.host, f"/{bucket}/{o['clave']}", {}, ak, sk,
                             body=data)
            if st not in (200, 201):
                problems.append(f"{o['clave']}: PUT gave HTTP {st}: {body[:120]!r}")
                continue
            st, _, headers = s3("HEAD", t.host, f"/{bucket}/{o['clave']}", {}, ak, sk)
            got = int(headers.get("Content-Length", -1)) if st == 200 else -1
            if got != len(data):
                problems.append(f"{o['clave']}: written, and the bucket returns "
                                f"{got} bytes where {len(data)} were expected")
                continue
            written += 1
            step(f"object {o['clave']}: {len(data)} bytes written")
        # And the count, which is the part a per-object loop cannot give:
        # an object that never got as far as a PUT leaves no trace in the
        # loop, and the listing is where its absence has a shape.
        alive = {x["clave"]: x["bytes"] for x in bucket_listing(t.host, bucket, ak, sk)}

    for o in p["objetos"]:
        if o["clave"] not in alive:
            problems.append(f"{o['clave']}: it is NOT in the bucket after restoring")
        elif alive[o["clave"]] != o["bytes"]:
            problems.append(f"{o['clave']}: {alive[o['clave']]} bytes in the bucket, "
                            f"{o['bytes']} were expected")
    if problems:
        for x in problems[:12]:
            print(f"  {red}✗{reset} {x}")
        die(f"{p['org']}/{bucket}: the objects do NOT reproduce what was captured "
            f"— I do not call it good")
    later = sorted(set(alive) - {o["clave"] for o in p["objetos"]})
    if later:
        print(f"  {yellow}!{reset} {len(later)} object(s) live in {bucket} and are not "
              f"in the bundle (later than the capture): "
              f"{', '.join(later[:5])} — they are NOT deleted")
    ok(f"{p['org']}/{bucket} restored and VERIFIED ({written} written, {identical} "
       f"already identical, {len(p['objetos'])} objects, "
       f"{sum(o['bytes'] for o in p['objetos'])} bytes)")


def restore(bundle, org, force):
    """The organization comes back in ONE window with its consumers down.

    The order matters, and it was wrong until the review of 2026-08-29:
    the loop walked the manifest's pieces, the manifest carries the
    databases first, so the postgres half opened the window and CLOSED it
    —`scale_consumers_up` in its `finally`— and only then did the objects
    go back into the bucket. The application was already serving, against
    rows pointing at objects still travelling: 404s in front of a user,
    over data the operator had just been told was restored.

    So the window is opened ONCE for the whole organization, and inside
    it the OBJECTS go first and the databases after. If that window ever
    closed halfway, an object without its row is an orphan nobody
    notices; a row without its object is the 404. The realignment of the
    role stays the last thing before the consumers come back, which is
    where it always had to be.
    """
    tmp, man = open_bundle(bundle)
    try:
        pieces = [p for p in man["piezas"] if p["org"] == org]
        if not pieces:
            die(f"the bundle has nothing of {org!r}. There is: "
                f"{', '.join(sorted({p['org'] for p in man['piezas']}))}")
        postgres = [p for p in pieces if p["tipo"] == "postgres"]
        mongos = [p for p in pieces if p["tipo"] == "mongodb"]
        buckets = [p for p in pieces if p["tipo"] not in ("postgres", "mongodb")]

        for p in postgres:
            # THE BRAKE, which since the realignment exists is no longer
            # about repair: the dump brings the role's SCRAM hash, and
            # what closes the gap between that hash and the live Secret
            # is the ALTER ROLE further down. What this fingerprint
            # detects is something the tool cannot decide on its own —
            # the credential is not the one from the capture, so either
            # it rotated or this bundle comes from ANOTHER instance, and
            # moving an organization into a new machine is not the same
            # operation as restoring one.
            live = credential_fingerprint(p["ns"], p["secreto"])
            expected = p.get("credencial_sha256")
            if expected and live and live != expected:
                msg = (f"the credential of {p['ns']}/{p['secreto']} is NOT the one "
                       f"that was there at capture time.\n"
                       f"      Either it rotated, or this bundle comes from ANOTHER "
                       f"instance — and the second case\n"
                       f"      deserves a person, because it is not the same thing to "
                       f"restore a machine as to move\n"
                       f"      an organization into a new one. The role is realigned "
                       f"with the live Secret\n"
                       f"      once the dump is loaded, so what is missing here is the "
                       f"decision, not the repair.")
                if not force:
                    die(msg)
                print(f"  {yellow}!{reset} {msg}\n      {yellow}--force: I go on anyway{reset}")

            # Every piece against its sha256 BEFORE a single write, which
            # is the order the objects follow too: a bundle that does not
            # reproduce itself is not applied halfway.
            for piece in [p["globals"]] + p["bases"]:
                b_ = open(os.path.join(tmp, piece["archivo"]), "rb").read()
                if hashlib.sha256(b_).hexdigest() != piece["sha256"]:
                    die(f"{piece['archivo']} does not match its sha256 from the manifest")

        # ── THE PROTOCOL, which is more than a `psql <` ─────────────
        #
        # The consumers are brought down because a pg_dump with --clean
        # drops objects the app may be using, and they stay down until
        # BOTH halves are back. They come up NO MATTER WHAT: if the
        # restore dies halfway, leaving the organization at zero replicas
        # would turn a failed restore into an outage.
        #
        # One organization is ONE namespace —`sources()` derives it that
        # way and the bucket piece never carried one— so the window is
        # opened once for the whole restore and not once per piece.
        ns = postgres[0]["ns"] if postgres else f"org-{org}"
        consumers = scale_consumers_down(ns)
        try:
            for p in buckets:
                restore_bucket(p, tmp)
            for p in postgres:
                pod = f"{p['servicio']}-0"
                cut_connections(p["ns"], pod)

                # Globals WITHOUT ON_ERROR_STOP: the roles already exist
                # and saying so is expected noise, not a failure.
                g = open(os.path.join(tmp, p["globals"]["archivo"]), "rb").read()
                kubectl(["exec", "-i", "-n", p["ns"], pod, "--", "sh", "-c",
                         'psql -U "$POSTGRES_USER" -d postgres -v ON_ERROR_STOP=0'],
                        binary=True, stdin_data=g)
                step(f"roles restored ({len(g)} bytes)")

                for database in p["bases"]:
                    alive_dbs = databases_of(p["ns"], pod)
                    if database["nombre"] not in alive_dbs:
                        psql(p["ns"], pod, "postgres",
                             "CREATE DATABASE "
                             + sql_identifier(database["nombre"]))
                        step(f"database {database['nombre']} did not exist: recreated")
                    sql = open(os.path.join(tmp, database["archivo"]), "rb").read()
                    r = kubectl(["exec", "-i", "-n", p["ns"], pod, "--", "sh", "-c",
                                 f'psql -U "$POSTGRES_USER" '
                                 f'-d {shq(database["nombre"])} '
                                 f'-v ON_ERROR_STOP=1'],
                                binary=True, stdin_data=sql)
                    err = (r.stderr or b"").decode(errors="replace")
                    serious = [l for l in err.splitlines() if "ERROR" in l]
                    if serious:
                        print(f"  {red}!{reset} psql over {database['nombre']}:")
                        for l in serious[:8]:
                            print(f"      {gray}{l}{reset}")
                    step(f"database {database['nombre']} applied ({len(sql)} bytes)")

                # ── THE ROLE, BACK IN STEP WITH THIS INSTANCE ───────
                #
                # Here, and not after bringing the consumers back up: the
                # pods return looking for the database, and finding it
                # with the password from the capture gives them a crash
                # loop whose message —authentication failed— points at the
                # application and not at the restore.
                #
                # It runs ALWAYS, including when the fingerprint matched:
                # setting the password the role already has costs one
                # statement, and the alternative is a branch that only
                # gets exercised on the day it matters.
                #
                # The database it proves the login against is the
                # organization's own (`POSTGRES_DB` is the organization's
                # name); if the bundle does not carry it, `postgres`,
                # which every server has.
                app_db = (p["org"] if any(b["nombre"] == p["org"] for b in p["bases"])
                          else "postgres")
                user = realign_role(p["ns"], pod, p["secreto"], app_db)
                step(f"role {user} realigned with {p['ns']}/{p['secreto']}: "
                     f"the live credential opens {app_db}")
            # mongodb: --drop --archive, one database at a time, feeding
            # the captured archive over stdin. No role to realign — the
            # root credential lives in the Secret the platform holds, and
            # mongorestore replaces DATA, not the admin user.
            for p in mongos:
                for base in p["bases"]:
                    archive = open(os.path.join(tmp, base["archivo"]), "rb").read()
                    r = _tmpl_run(p["tmpl"]["restore"], base["nombre"], stdin_data=archive)
                    if r.returncode != 0:
                        die(f"the mongorestore of {base['nombre']} failed: "
                            f"{(r.stderr or b'').decode(errors='replace')[:200]}")
                    step(f"mongodb {base['nombre']} restored ({len(archive)} bytes)")
        finally:
            scale_consumers_up(ns, consumers)

        # ── VERIFICATION, the part that was missing ─────────────────
        #
        # On 2026-08-09 a restore left the database DELETED and reported
        # success, because it looked at psql's exit code and not at the
        # result. Now the inventory is measured again and compared
        # against the one stored at capture time. If it does not match,
        # it is not restored, whatever psql says.
        #
        # It runs with the consumers already back up, and that is not an
        # oversight: what is measured here is the content of the tables,
        # which a returning application does not change. The bucket
        # measures itself inside the window, because its own listing —the
        # only thing that can answer for it— is read there.
        for p in postgres:
            pod = f"{p['servicio']}-0"
            problems = []
            alive_dbs = databases_of(p["ns"], pod)
            for database in p["bases"]:
                if database["nombre"] not in alive_dbs:
                    problems.append(f"the database {database['nombre']} DOES NOT EXIST")
                    continue
                now = {t["tabla"]: t["filas"] for t in (inventory(p["ns"], pod, database["nombre"]) or [])}
                for t in database["tablas"] or []:
                    if t["tabla"] not in now:
                        problems.append(f"{database['nombre']}: the table {t['tabla']} is missing")
                    elif now[t["tabla"]] != t["filas"]:
                        problems.append(
                            f"{database['nombre']}.{t['tabla']}: {now[t['tabla']]} rows, "
                            f"{t['filas']} were expected")
            if problems:
                for x in problems[:12]:
                    print(f"  {red}✗{reset} {x}")
                die(f"{p['org']}/{p['servicio']}: the restore does NOT reproduce what "
                    f"was captured — I do not call it good")
            n_t = sum(len(b_["tablas"] or []) for b_ in p["bases"])
            ok(f"{p['org']}/{p['servicio']} restored and VERIFIED "
               f"({len(p['bases'])} databases, {n_t} tables, the rows match)")
        # The same measurement for mongodb: the collections and their
        # document counts, read back and compared against capture. A
        # mongorestore that returns 0 over a database it did not touch is
        # exactly the 2026-08-09 lie in another engine.
        for p in mongos:
            problems = []
            alive = mongo_databases(p)
            for base in p["bases"]:
                if base["nombre"] not in alive:
                    problems.append(f"the database {base['nombre']} DOES NOT EXIST")
                    continue
                now = {c["coleccion"]: c["docs"] for c in (mongo_inventory(p, base["nombre"]) or [])}
                for c in base.get("colecciones") or []:
                    if c["coleccion"] not in now:
                        problems.append(f"{base['nombre']}: the collection {c['coleccion']} is missing")
                    elif now[c["coleccion"]] != c["docs"]:
                        problems.append(
                            f"{base['nombre']}.{c['coleccion']}: {now[c['coleccion']]} docs, "
                            f"{c['docs']} were expected")
            if problems:
                for x in problems[:12]:
                    print(f"  {red}✗{reset} {x}")
                die(f"{p['org']}/{p['servicio']}: the mongodb restore does NOT reproduce "
                    f"what was captured — I do not call it good")
            n_c = sum(len(b_.get("colecciones") or []) for b_ in p["bases"])
            ok(f"{p['org']}/{p['servicio']} restored and VERIFIED "
               f"({len(p['bases'])} database(s), {n_c} collection(s), the counts match)")
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(tmp, ignore_errors=True)
    return 0


# ── how much it weighs ──────────────────────────────────────────────
def sizes(only_org=None):
    """What each organization's data weighs, asked of whoever knows.

    The instance's storage class is local-path: it neither measures nor
    limits the disk a PVC really uses. `kubectl` gives back the size that
    was REQUESTED, which is a promise and not a measurement, so until
    this existed the only honest answer to «how much does this
    organization weigh?» was to open the pod by hand. A number nobody can
    obtain is a number nobody watches, and the first sign of a full disk
    is postgres refusing to write.

    So it is asked of the two that do know: postgres about its databases
    (`pg_database_size`) and the bucket's own listing about its objects.
    Both are cheap — the listing already carries every object's size, so
    a bucket costs one request per thousand objects and not one per
    object.

    It is a FUNCTION and not only a verb of the CLI because whoever
    watches the platform has to consume the NUMBER, not parse a sentence:
    `metrics()` turns this into series, and where they are pushed is the
    caller's decision.
    """
    databases, mongos, buckets = sources()
    measured = []
    for b in databases:
        if only_org and b["org"] != only_org:
            continue
        bases = []
        for database in databases_of(b["ns"], b["pod"]):
            n, rc = psql(b["ns"], b["pod"], "postgres",
                         f"SELECT pg_database_size({sql_literal(database)})")
            bases.append({"nombre": database,
                          "bytes": int(n) if rc == 0 and n.isdigit() else -1})
        # What did not answer is left OUT of the total and is not counted
        # as a zero: a zero would make a failed measurement look like an
        # empty database. So the total is a FLOOR, each database that did
        # not answer goes out as -1 in its own series, and `measure`
        # names them — a sum that quietly drops what it could not measure
        # is the same lie as a restore that reports success.
        measured.append({"tipo": "postgres", "org": b["org"], "ns": b["ns"],
                         "servicio": b["servicio"], "bases": bases,
                         "bytes": sum(x["bytes"] for x in bases if x["bytes"] >= 0)})
    for m in mongos:
        if only_org and m["org"] != only_org:
            continue
        bases = [{"nombre": name, "bytes": b_} for name, b_ in mongo_sizes(m)]
        measured.append({"tipo": "mongodb", "org": m["org"], "ns": m["ns"],
                         "servicio": m["servicio"], "bases": bases,
                         "bytes": sum(x["bytes"] for x in bases if x["bytes"] >= 0)})
    for bk in buckets:
        if only_org and bk["org"] != only_org:
            continue
        ak, sk = s3_credential(bk["ns"])
        with Tunnel("garage-system", "garage", 3900) as t:
            objects = bucket_listing(t.host, bk["bucket"], ak, sk)
        measured.append({"tipo": "bucket", "org": bk["org"], "ns": bk["ns"],
                         "bucket": bk["bucket"], "objetos": len(objects),
                         "bytes": sum(o["bytes"] for o in objects if o["bytes"] >= 0)})
    return measured


def label_value(value):
    """A label value in the exposition format: `\\`, `"` and the line break.

    It is not decoration. Of the four labels emitted here, three come out
    of a contract and go through lib/aegis/org.py's
    `^[a-z][a-z0-9-]{2,29}$`; `database` comes out of the LIVE SERVER,
    where the tenant —superuser of its own instance— names it. A quote in
    it does not spoil one series: it makes the LINE malformed, and
    /api/v1/import/prometheus rejects the whole batch, so one
    organization's database would silence the measurement of every
    organization. A line break is worse: the series splits in two.

    The three validated ones go through here as well, on purpose: an
    escape that depends on remembering who validated what is an escape
    that will be forgotten.
    """
    return value.replace("\\", "\\\\").replace('"', '\\"').replace("\n", "\\n")


def metrics(measured):
    """The measurement in the exposition format the rest of the platform speaks.

    Same convention as the producers that already live in the seed
    (`aegis_image_watch_*`, `aegis_trivy_db_updated_timestamp_seconds`):
    the `aegis_` prefix, the unit at the end of the name, and the identity
    in LABELS and never in the name — a metric per organization would be a
    metric nobody can aggregate.

    And a timestamp beside the figures, which is what lets a rule notice
    that the measurement went SILENT: a gauge that stops being written
    keeps its last value for ever, and stale is indistinguishable from
    healthy without something dated to compare against. It is the same
    pair the image watch uses, and for the same reason.

    It returns lines and pushes nothing. Where they go —a host timer, the
    round of checks— is decided outside: this command does not reach into
    another one.
    """
    now = int(time.time())
    lines, orgs = [], []
    for m in measured:
        if m["org"] not in orgs:
            orgs.append(m["org"])
        # Every label value goes out through `label_value`, one by one and
        # not through a variable prepared above: the rule «a label is
        # escaped where it is written» is one a reader can check at a
        # glance, and check 154 measures exactly that.
        if m["tipo"] in ("postgres", "mongodb"):
            for b in m["bases"]:
                lines.append(
                    f'aegis_org_database_bytes{{org="{label_value(m["org"])}",'
                    f'service="{label_value(m["servicio"])}",'
                    f'motor="{label_value(m["tipo"])}",'
                    f'database="{label_value(b["nombre"])}"}} {b["bytes"]}')
        else:
            lines.append(
                f'aegis_org_bucket_bytes{{org="{label_value(m["org"])}",'
                f'bucket="{label_value(m["bucket"])}"}} {m["bytes"]}')
            lines.append(
                f'aegis_org_bucket_objects{{org="{label_value(m["org"])}",'
                f'bucket="{label_value(m["bucket"])}"}} {m["objetos"]}')
    for org in orgs:
        lines.append(f'aegis_org_data_measured_timestamp_seconds'
                     f'{{org="{label_value(org)}"}} {now}')
    return lines


def measure(only_org):
    """The measurement on STDOUT, ready to be piped where it is collected.

    stdout carries ONLY the series — nothing else — so the output goes
    straight into whoever ingests it without a filter in between. What a
    person reads goes to stderr. It is the same rule the rest of the
    product follows with anything whose value gets captured.
    """
    measured = sizes(only_org)
    if not measured:
        die("no organization to measure" + (f": {only_org!r} is not one of the "
                                            f"contracts" if only_org else ""))
    for m in measured:
        if m["tipo"] in ("postgres", "mongodb"):
            blind = [x["nombre"] for x in m["bases"] if x["bytes"] < 0]
            print(f"  {gray}·{reset} {m['tipo']} {m['ns']}/{m['servicio']}: {m['bytes']} "
                  f"bytes in {len(m['bases']) - len(blind)} databases",
                  file=sys.stderr)
            if blind:
                print(f"  {yellow}!{reset} {m['ns']}/{m['servicio']}: {len(blind)} "
                      f"database(s) did not answer their size "
                      f"({', '.join(blind[:5])}) — the figure above is a FLOOR "
                      f"and not the weight", file=sys.stderr)
        else:
            print(f"  {gray}·{reset} bucket   {m['bucket']}: {m['bytes']} bytes in "
                  f"{m['objetos']} objects", file=sys.stderr)
    for line in metrics(measured):
        print(line)
    return 0


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  THE OFF-SITE DESTINATION, from the operator's side
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
def remote_adopt(credentials_file):
    """Takes the destination's S3 pair into the encrypted store.

    THE PAIR NEVER COMES THROUGH argv. `/proc/PID/cmdline` is
    world-readable, so a key handed on the command line is a key handed
    to every process on the machine; it arrives in a file (in tmpfs
    when the init is the one calling) with two lines: the access key id
    and the secret.

    AND IT IS TESTED BEFORE IT IS STORED, against the real destination
    and not against its shape. That test tells apart the three states
    that a shape check collapses into one:

      · it lists          — the credential works and the bucket is there.
      · NoSuchBucket      — the credential works and the tofu of the
                            bucket has not been applied. It IS stored,
                            with the missing step named: a token minted
                            in phase 15 for a bucket created afterwards
                            is the normal order of things.
      · 401/403           — it is not stored. A credential that does not
                            open the destination, sitting in the store,
                            is an off-site copy that exists on paper and
                            fails on the day of the catastrophe.
    """
    if not os.path.isfile(credentials_file):
        die(f"{credentials_file} does not exist: the pair is read from a "
            f"file and never from the command line, because argv is "
            f"world-readable in /proc")
    lines = [l.strip() for l in open(credentials_file, encoding="utf-8").read()
             .splitlines() if l.strip()]
    if len(lines) != 2:
        die(f"{credentials_file} does not carry exactly two lines (access key "
            f"id, then secret access key): {len(lines)} were read")
    key_id, key_secret = lines
    # The SHAPE, measured on 2026-08-29 against
    # developers.cloudflare.com/r2/api/tokens: R2 derives the pair from
    # an API token, where the access key id is the token's `id` (32 hex)
    # and the secret is the sha256 of the token's value (64 hex). It is a
    # WARNING and not a refusal, because the format belongs to a third
    # party and may change — the verdict is given by the request below,
    # which is the only thing that measures what matters.
    if not (re.fullmatch(r"[0-9a-f]{32}", key_id)
            and re.fullmatch(r"[0-9a-f]{64}", key_secret)):
        print(f"  {yellow}!{reset} the pair does not have R2's measured shape "
              f"(32 hex id, 64 hex secret). It goes on to the real test all "
              f"the same: the format belongs to a third party, the answer of "
              f"the destination does not", file=sys.stderr)
    cfg = remote_config()
    st, body, _ = remote_s3("GET", "", query={"list-type": "2", "max-keys": "1"},
                            cfg=cfg, pair=(key_id, key_secret))
    if st in (401, 403):
        die(f"the destination REJECTED that pair (HTTP {st}) and nothing was "
            f"stored: {body[:160]!r}.\n"
            f"      A credential that does not open {cfg['bucket']}, sitting "
            f"in the store, is an off-site copy that exists on paper and "
            f"fails on the day it is needed")
    if st == 404:
        print(f"  {yellow}!{reset} the credential is accepted and the bucket "
              f"{cfg['bucket']} does NOT exist yet at the destination. The "
              f"pair IS stored — that is the normal order: the token is "
              f"minted with the master credential and the bucket is created "
              f"afterwards with tofu (envs/data-r2). Until that apply, every "
              f"backup will report that it did not leave the machine",
              file=sys.stderr)
    elif st != 200:
        die(f"the destination answered HTTP {st} to a listing "
            f"({body[:160]!r}) and nothing was stored: what could not be "
            f"measured is not adopted")
    store_write(REMOTE_ID_NAME, key_id.encode())
    store_write(REMOTE_KEY_NAME, key_secret.encode())
    ok(f"off-site destination adopted: {cfg['bucket']} at {cfg['host']} "
       f"(the pair went into the encrypted store, never into argv)")
    # The gap this command opens and cannot close by itself, said out
    # loud rather than left for somebody to find: `aegis rotate` builds
    # its menu from a table of recipes, and there is none for these two
    # names yet. Its inventory will list them under «material with NO
    # RECIPE», which is correct and must not be silenced.
    print(f"  {gray}·{reset} {cli.cmd('rotate')} does not know how to rotate "
          f"{REMOTE_ID_NAME}/{REMOTE_KEY_NAME} yet and will say so: the "
          f"rotation of this pair is section 7 of the backups protocol, by "
          f"hand, until a recipe exists", file=sys.stderr)
    return 0


def remote_push_bundle(bundle, prefix):
    """Sends out a bundle this command did not make.

    It exists for the OTHER half. `aegis state backup` writes the
    platform's bundle —the store's secrets, the init's trail, the edge's
    tfstate— into the same tree, and it does not know how to send it
    anywhere: with the data and without the state you have a dump nobody
    knows where to put. Until that command learns the road, this verb is
    the road, and the protocol says to use it.

    The prefix is asked for and not guessed: what this receives is a
    file, and a file does not say which organization it belongs to. The
    same guard as every other exit applies — age's header or nothing.
    """
    with open(bundle, "rb") as f:
        if f.read(len(AGE_MAGIC)) != AGE_MAGIC:
            die(f"{os.path.basename(bundle)} is NOT an age-encrypted file and "
                f"NOTHING was uploaded: only ciphertext leaves this machine")
    cfg = remote_config()
    pair = remote_credential()
    data = open(bundle, "rb").read()
    key = f"{prefix}/{os.path.basename(bundle)}"
    st, body, _ = remote_s3("PUT", key, body=data, timeout=600, cfg=cfg, pair=pair)
    if st not in (200, 201):
        die(f"{key} did NOT leave the machine: HTTP {st} ({body[:160]!r})")
    st, _, headers = remote_s3("HEAD", key, cfg=cfg, pair=pair)
    got = int(headers.get("Content-Length", -1)) if st == 200 else -1
    if got != len(data):
        die(f"{key}: uploaded, and the destination returns {got} bytes where "
            f"{len(data)} were expected — it is not called good")
    side = remote_sidecar(bundle, prefix)
    st, body, _ = remote_s3("PUT", key + ".manifiesto.json",
                            body=json.dumps(side, indent=2,
                                            ensure_ascii=False).encode(),
                            cfg=cfg, pair=pair)
    if st not in (200, 201):
        die(f"{key} went up and its manifest did NOT ({st})")
    ok(f"off-site: {cfg['bucket']}/{key} ({len(data)} bytes) and its manifest")
    return 0


def remote_inventory():
    """What is at the destination, grouped by organization.

    An object with no `.manifiesto.json` beside it is NAMED and not
    counted: it is a bundle that cannot be checked before restoring,
    which is the state the whole upload path exists to avoid, and
    hiding it in a total would make an unusable copy look like a copy.
    """
    cfg = remote_config()
    objects = remote_listing(cfg=cfg)
    bundles, sidecars = {}, set()
    for o in objects:
        if o["clave"].endswith(".manifiesto.json"):
            sidecars.add(o["clave"][: -len(".manifiesto.json")])
            continue
        org = o["clave"].split("/", 1)[0]
        org = org[4:] if org.startswith("org-") else org
        bundles.setdefault(org, []).append(o)
    orphans = [o["clave"] for v in bundles.values() for o in v
               if o["clave"] not in sidecars]
    return cfg, bundles, orphans


def remote_epoch(stamp):
    """The date of an S3 listing, as an epoch. None if it is unreadable.

    S3 answers ISO 8601 in UTC with a `Z` and, depending on the
    implementation, with or without fractions of a second. It is parsed
    and NOT assumed: an unreadable date has to come out as None so that
    whoever measures the freshness reports COULD NOT EVALUATE instead of
    a zero, which would read as «1970» and page for a healthy
    destination.
    """
    if not stamp:
        return None
    s = stamp.strip().replace("Z", "+00:00")
    try:
        return datetime.datetime.fromisoformat(s).timestamp()
    except ValueError:
        return None


def remote_list():
    cfg, bundles, orphans = remote_inventory()
    print(f"{gray}destination:{reset} {cfg['bucket']}  {gray}at{reset} {cfg['host']}\n",
          file=sys.stderr)
    if not bundles:
        print(f"  {yellow}!{reset} the destination is EMPTY: this instance has "
              f"never sent a bundle out", file=sys.stderr)
    now = time.time()
    for org in sorted(bundles):
        items = sorted(bundles[org], key=lambda o: o["clave"])
        newest = max((remote_epoch(o["modificado"]) or 0) for o in items)
        age = (now - newest) / 3600.0 if newest else -1
        total = sum(o["bytes"] for o in items if o["bytes"] >= 0)
        print(f"  {gray}·{reset} org-{org}: {len(items)} bundle(s), {total} bytes, "
              f"the newest {age:.1f} h ago" if age >= 0 else
              f"  {gray}·{reset} org-{org}: {len(items)} bundle(s), {total} bytes, "
              f"date UNREADABLE", file=sys.stderr)
        for o in items[-3:]:
            print(f"      {gray}{o['clave']}  {o['bytes']} B{reset}", file=sys.stderr)
    for c in orphans:
        print(f"  {red}✗{reset} {c} has NO manifest at the destination: it "
              f"cannot be checked before restoring, so it does not count as a "
              f"copy", file=sys.stderr)
    return 1 if orphans else 0


def remote_status():
    """The freshness of the destination, as series on stdout.

    Same convention as `size`: stdout carries ONLY the metrics, the
    prose goes to stderr, and the timestamp travels beside the figures
    so that whoever watches can tell a measurement that STOPPED from
    one that is healthy — a gauge that is no longer written keeps its
    last value for ever.

    The verdict follows share/exit-codes.txt, and the 2 is the one that
    matters: a destination that cannot be reached is NOT a destination
    with no backup. Reporting it as 1 would send somebody to look for a
    backup that is probably right there, behind a network that is down.
    """
    try:
        cfg, bundles, orphans = remote_inventory()
    except cli.CouldNotEvaluate as e:
        print(f"  {yellow}!{reset} {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    now = int(time.time())
    lines, stale = [], []
    for org in sorted(bundles):
        items = bundles[org]
        epochs = [e for e in (remote_epoch(o["modificado"]) for o in items) if e]
        total = sum(o["bytes"] for o in items if o["bytes"] >= 0)
        lines.append(f'aegis_backup_remote_bundles{{org="{label_value(org)}"}} '
                     f'{len(items)}')
        lines.append(f'aegis_backup_remote_bytes{{org="{label_value(org)}"}} {total}')
        if epochs:
            lines.append(f'aegis_backup_remote_timestamp_seconds'
                         f'{{org="{label_value(org)}"}} {int(max(epochs))}')
        else:
            stale.append(org)
    lines.append(f"aegis_backup_remote_measured_timestamp_seconds {now}")
    # The clock, exported beside the ages it has to be read against.
    # Without it an alert on staleness has to hard-code a threshold,
    # and the day somebody sets the cadence to 6h the alert keeps
    # judging against a number nobody agreed to. It is read from the
    # machine, never assumed: `cadence_current` looks at the drop-in
    # and falls back to what the unit ships.
    cad_text, _cad_src = cadence_current()
    cad_s = parse_duration(cad_text)
    if cad_s:
        lines.append(f"aegis_backup_remote_cadence_seconds {cad_s}")
    for org in sorted(bundles):
        epochs = [e for e in (remote_epoch(o["modificado"]) for o in bundles[org]) if e]
        age = (now - max(epochs)) / 3600.0 if epochs else None
        print(f"  {gray}·{reset} org-{org}: newest copy "
              f"{'%.1f h ago' % age if age is not None else 'of an UNREADABLE date'}",
              file=sys.stderr)
    for org in stale:
        print(f"  {yellow}!{reset} org-{org}: no readable date at the "
              f"destination — its freshness is NOT measured, which is not the "
              f"same as being fresh", file=sys.stderr)
    for c in orphans:
        print(f"  {red}✗{reset} {c} has no manifest: it does not count as a copy",
              file=sys.stderr)
    for line in lines:
        print(line)
    if not bundles:
        print(f"  {yellow}!{reset} the destination is empty: nothing has ever "
              f"left this machine", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    return 1 if (stale or orphans) else 0


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  THE CLOCK, and the door that a terminal alone cannot open
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# A backup with no clock is a backup somebody remembers. Measured on
# 2026-08-29 on the live instance: the newest bundle was three days old
# and had been made by hand. The timer that closes that lives in
# share/systemd/; what lives HERE is the only thing a unit file cannot
# hold — the reason a cadence is or is not acceptable.
#
# THE FLOOR IS ONE HOUR, and it is not about the disk. Every run of the
# capture opens a port-forward per bucket, runs a pg_dumpall and a
# pg_dump per database against the live server, downloads every object,
# and pushes the whole bundle to a third party. Under an hour that stops
# being a backup and becomes a load: on the postgres of every tenant, on
# the household connection, and on a free tier that is counted in
# operations as well as in bytes.
#
# So below an hour the command asks for a SECOND CHANNEL, and the shape
# of that is deliberate. A confirmation typed into the terminal is a
# confirmation anything holding the terminal can give — an agent, a
# script, a paste. A code that arrives on the operator's phone and has
# to be typed back cannot be produced by whoever is at the keyboard: it
# has to be READ somewhere else. That is the whole property, and it is
# why the code is never printed, never logged and never written to a
# file on this machine.
#
# And if the second channel is not configured, the answer is NO. It does
# not fall back to «type y to confirm»: a brake that yields when its
# mechanism is missing was never a brake.
CADENCE_FLOOR_S = 3600
CADENCE_CEILING_S = 24 * 3600
CADENCE_DEFAULT = "24h"
CADENCE_UNIT = "aegis-backup.timer"
NTFY_TOPIC = "aegis-alertas"
NTFY_USER = "puente"


def cadence_dropin_path():
    """Where the cadence override lives, and why it is a USER unit.

    The capture runs as the operator and not as root, for the reason
    this file's opening argues at length: it needs the age key, the
    kubeconfig and a `kubectl exec` into every tenant. So the timer is a
    user unit, its drop-in lives under the operator's own configuration,
    and changing the cadence needs no privilege at all — which is what
    lets this command write it instead of printing a recipe for somebody
    to paste wrong.
    """
    base = os.environ.get("XDG_CONFIG_HOME") or os.path.expanduser("~/.config")
    return os.path.join(base, "systemd", "user", f"{CADENCE_UNIT}.d",
                        "aegis-cadence.conf")


def parse_duration(text):
    """A systemd-shaped duration into seconds. None if it is not one.

    Only the three units a backup cadence can sensibly use are accepted.
    Being liberal here would mean accepting something systemd reads
    DIFFERENTLY —`1` is one second for systemd and would look like one
    hour to a person— and the drop-in would then say something other
    than what was confirmed.
    """
    m = re.fullmatch(r"(\d+)\s*(s|m|h)", (text or "").strip())
    if not m:
        return None
    return int(m.group(1)) * {"s": 1, "m": 60, "h": 3600}[m.group(2)]


def cadence_dropin(text):
    """The systemd drop-in that changes the cadence.

    The EMPTY assignment before the value is not decoration: in systemd
    a drop-in ADDS to a list-valued setting, so an `OnUnitActiveSec=6h`
    on its own leaves the shipped 24h in place and the timer fires on
    BOTH. Resetting the list first is the only way to replace it, and
    forgetting it produces a timer that looks configured and runs twice.
    """
    return (f"# written by `{cli.cmd('data')} remote cadence --set {text}`.\n"
            f"# The empty assignment RESETS the list: without it this value is\n"
            f"# added to the unit's own and the timer fires on both.\n"
            f"[Timer]\n"
            f"OnUnitActiveSec=\n"
            f"OnUnitActiveSec={text}\n")


def cadence_current():
    """What the machine has installed, read and not assumed."""
    path = cadence_dropin_path()
    if os.path.isfile(path):
        for line in open(path, encoding="utf-8"):
            if line.startswith("OnUnitActiveSec=") and line.split("=", 1)[1].strip():
                return line.split("=", 1)[1].strip(), path
    return CADENCE_DEFAULT, f"the shipped {CADENCE_UNIT} (no drop-in)"


def cadence_cost(seconds):
    """What a cadence costs, in the units the far side actually counts.

    It is an ESTIMATE and it says so: the size comes from what is at the
    destination today, and an organization that grows changes the answer.
    What it is NOT is a guess — the figures come from a listing.
    """
    runs = 86400.0 / seconds
    try:
        _, bundles, _ = remote_inventory()
    except cli.CouldNotEvaluate:
        bundles = {}
    orgs = max(len(bundles), 1)
    sizes = [o["bytes"] for v in bundles.values() for o in v if o["bytes"] >= 0]
    typical = max(sizes) if sizes else 0
    # 2 writes per organization per run: the bundle and its manifest.
    return {"runs": runs, "orgs": orgs, "bundle_bytes": typical,
            "writes_month": runs * orgs * 2 * 30,
            "bytes_month": runs * orgs * typical * 30}


def ntfy_publish(title, message):
    """One message to the operator's phone, over the platform's own channel.

    ntfy is self-hosted for the reason design.md gives — the «the
    platform is broken» channel cannot depend on a free service with
    guessable topics — and it is deny-all: the `puente` user is the one
    with write on this topic, and its credential lives in the store.

    The port-forward is the same road the capture takes to Garage and
    for the same reason: the Service is ClusterIP and the operator is
    not a namespace of the cluster, but the traffic is born on the node.
    The credential travels in a header, never in argv.
    """
    token = store_read("ntfy_bridge_token")
    if token is None:
        raise cli.CouldNotEvaluate(
            "there is no ntfy credential in this instance's store: the "
            "second channel does not exist here")
    pair = NTFY_USER.encode() + b":" + token.strip()
    with Tunnel("observability", "ntfy", 80) as t:
        req = urllib.request.Request(
            f"http://{t.host}/{NTFY_TOPIC}", data=message.encode(),
            headers={"Title": title, "Priority": "high",
                     "Authorization": "Basic " + base64.b64encode(pair).decode()},
            method="POST")
        try:
            with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=20) as r:
                if r.status not in (200, 201):
                    raise cli.CouldNotEvaluate(f"ntfy answered HTTP {r.status}")
        except urllib.error.HTTPError as e:
            raise cli.CouldNotEvaluate(
                f"ntfy rejected the publication (HTTP {e.code}): the second "
                f"channel is not usable")
        except OSError as e:
            raise cli.CouldNotEvaluate(f"ntfy is not reachable: {e}")


def cadence_record(text, seconds, channel):
    """Who confirmed, when, and through what. Never the code.

    It goes beside the init's trail because that is where this instance
    keeps what it did to itself, and it is append-only for the same
    reason gates.jsonl is: a decision that can be edited afterwards is
    not a record.
    """
    d = paths.state_dir()
    d.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    entry = {
        "cuando": datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc)
                  .strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ"),
        "quien": os.environ.get("SUDO_USER") or getpass.getuser(),
        "cadencia": text,
        "segundos": seconds,
        "canal": channel,
    }
    with open(d / "backup-cadence.jsonl", "a", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        f.write(json.dumps(entry, ensure_ascii=False) + "\n")
    return entry


def cadence_gate(text, seconds):
    """The door below one hour. It opens with a code that is not on this machine.

    Three refusals, in this order, and none of them degrades into the
    next:

      1. NO OPERATOR. With AEGIS_NONINTERACTIVE, or with stdin not a
         terminal, there is nobody to read a phone. Answering «assume
         yes» here would make the whole gate a comment.
      2. NO SECOND CHANNEL. If ntfy is not configured, the code has
         nowhere to go, and asking for a confirmation typed at the same
         keyboard would be theatre.
      3. THE CODE. Six digits, generated with the random source meant
         for secrets, sent to the phone and never shown here. It is
         typed back beside the word `entendido` — the word is not
         decoration: it is what stops a code pasted by reflex from being
         a confirmation of something that was not read.
    """
    if os.environ.get("AEGIS_NONINTERACTIVE") or not sys.stdin.isatty():
        die(f"a cadence under {CADENCE_FLOOR_S // 60} minutes needs a "
            f"confirmation the operator gives on their PHONE, and there is no "
            f"operator here (non-interactive). The cadence is NOT changed. "
            f"This is the answer on purpose: a brake that yields when nobody "
            f"is watching is not a brake")
    cost = cadence_cost(seconds)
    print(f"\n  {yellow}a cadence of {text} means, per month and at today's "
          f"size:{reset}", file=sys.stderr)
    print(f"    {cost['runs']:.1f} runs a day over {cost['orgs']} "
          f"organization(s)", file=sys.stderr)
    print(f"    ~{cost['writes_month']:.0f} writes at the destination "
          f"(the free tier counts operations, not only bytes)", file=sys.stderr)
    print(f"    ~{cost['bytes_month'] / 1e9:.2f} GB uploaded, over a household "
          f"connection, against a 10 GB shelf", file=sys.stderr)
    print(f"    and one pg_dump per database, per run, against the live "
          f"server of every tenant", file=sys.stderr)
    print(f"  {gray}an estimate from the bundles that are at the destination "
          f"today; an organization that grows changes it{reset}\n",
          file=sys.stderr)
    code = f"{onetime.randbelow(1000000):06d}"
    try:
        ntfy_publish(
            "aegis: backup cadence below one hour",
            f"Somebody at the keyboard of this instance is asking to back up "
            f"every {text}. If it was you, type this code beside the word "
            f"entendido: {code}")
    except cli.CouldNotEvaluate as e:
        die(f"a cadence under {CADENCE_FLOOR_S // 60} minutes needs a second "
            f"channel and this instance does not have one: {e}.\n"
            f"      It does NOT fall back to a confirmation typed here: the "
            f"whole point is that the answer cannot be produced by whoever "
            f"holds this terminal. The cadence is not changed")
    print(f"  a code went to the phone on the {NTFY_TOPIC} topic. It is not "
          f"shown here and it is not written to any file on this machine.",
          file=sys.stderr)
    try:
        typed = input("  type: entendido <code>  ")
    except (EOFError, KeyboardInterrupt):
        die("no answer: the cadence is not changed")
    parts = typed.strip().split()
    if len(parts) != 2 or parts[0].lower() != "entendido" or parts[1] != code:
        die("that is not the code, or the word is missing. The cadence is "
            "NOT changed, and the code just used is dead: ask again")
    return cadence_record(text, seconds, f"ntfy/{NTFY_TOPIC}")


def remote_cadence(set_to):
    """Shows the clock, or changes it — with the floor in the way.

    It PRINTS the drop-in instead of installing it, and that is not
    timidity: writing under /etc/systemd/system needs root, and a
    command that asks for root in order to change a number would be a
    command that runs as root every time it is used. What cannot be
    obtained without passing through here is the TEXT for a cadence
    under an hour.
    """
    current, where = cadence_current()
    if set_to is None:
        print(f"  {gray}·{reset} cadence: {current}  {gray}({where}){reset}",
              file=sys.stderr)
        print(f"  {gray}·{reset} floor {CADENCE_FLOOR_S // 3600}h, ceiling "
              f"{CADENCE_CEILING_S // 3600}h; under the floor a confirmation "
              f"on the phone is required", file=sys.stderr)
        return 0
    seconds = parse_duration(set_to)
    if seconds is None:
        die(f"{set_to!r} is not a duration this command accepts (`30m`, `6h`, "
            f"`3600s`). It is deliberately narrow: systemd reads a bare number "
            f"as SECONDS, and a drop-in that says something other than what "
            f"was confirmed is worse than no drop-in")
    if seconds > CADENCE_CEILING_S:
        die(f"{set_to} is over the ceiling of {CADENCE_CEILING_S // 3600}h. A "
            f"day is already the most this instance may lose, and a cadence "
            f"that does not fit in a day is a backup you find out about later "
            f"than the disaster")
    if seconds < CADENCE_FLOOR_S:
        entry = cadence_gate(set_to, seconds)
        ok(f"confirmed on the second channel by {entry['quien']} at "
           f"{entry['cuando']} — recorded in the instance's trail")
    else:
        cadence_record(set_to, seconds, "terminal")
    path = cadence_dropin_path()
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
    with open(path, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        f.write(cadence_dropin(set_to))
    ok(f"cadence {current} -> {set_to}  ({path})")
    print(f"  {gray}·{reset} it takes effect on the next reload:", file=sys.stderr)
    print(f"  {gray}    systemctl --user daemon-reload && systemctl --user "
          f"restart {CADENCE_UNIT}{reset}", file=sys.stderr)
    return 0


def remote_main(a):
    if a.remote_verb == "bucket":
        # stdout carries the NAME and nothing else: this is what the
        # protocol pipes into TF_VAR_backups_bucket, and a line of prose
        # in there would create a bucket with a decorated name.
        print(remote_config()["bucket"])
        return 0
    if a.remote_verb == "adopt":
        return remote_adopt(a.credentials_file)
    if a.remote_verb == "push":
        return remote_push_bundle(a.bundle, a.prefix)
    if a.remote_verb == "list":
        return remote_list()
    if a.remote_verb == "status":
        return remote_status()
    return remote_cadence(a.cadence)


def main():
    # Rule 1: verbs, not flags. And the same pair of names as
    # `aegis state`, because they are the same operation over another
    # thing: `state` is the MACHINE, `data` is the tenants' DATA.
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog=cli.cmd("data"))
    sub = ap.add_subparsers(dest="verb", required=True)
    s = sub.add_parser("backup", help=f"one bundle per organization (default: {DEFAULT_DEST})")
    s.add_argument("directory", nargs="?", default="", metavar="DIRECTORY")
    s.add_argument("--no-remote", action="store_true",
                   help="do not send it out. For a capture taken to look at, "
                        "not to survive with")
    s = sub.add_parser("list", help="what is inside a bundle, without restoring")
    s.add_argument("bundle", metavar="BUNDLE.age")
    s = sub.add_parser("restore", help="restores the data of ONE organization")
    s.add_argument("bundle", metavar="BUNDLE.age",
                   help="a local path, or the key at the destination with "
                        "--from-remote")
    s.add_argument("--from-remote", action="store_true",
                   help="the bundle is brought down from the off-site "
                        "destination and checked against its manifest before "
                        "anything is touched")
    s.add_argument("--org", required=True,
                   help="restoring EVERYTHING in one go is not an operation worth making easy")
    s.add_argument("--force", action="store_true",
                    help="go on even if the credential is not the one from the capture")
    # `size` and not a flag of `backup`: measuring is not a step of
    # capturing. It is asked for on its own —from a timer, from a round
    # of checks— and it writes nothing.
    s = sub.add_parser("size", help="what the data weighs, as metrics on stdout")
    s.add_argument("--org", default=None, metavar="ORG",
                   help="only this organization (default: all of them)")
    # `remote` and not a flag: the off-site destination is a subject of
    # its own —it is adopted, listed, measured and given a clock— and
    # every one of those verbs answers a different question than
    # «capture». A flag would have made them options of an operation
    # they are not part of.
    s = sub.add_parser("remote", help="the OFF-SITE destination: where the bundle goes")
    rsub = s.add_subparsers(dest="remote_verb", required=True)
    rsub.add_parser("bucket", help="the destination's name, derived (stdout)")
    r = rsub.add_parser("adopt", help="takes the destination's S3 pair into the store")
    r.add_argument("--credentials-file", required=True, metavar="FILE",
                   help="two lines: access key id and secret access key. A "
                        "file and never argv, which is world-readable in /proc")
    r = rsub.add_parser("push", help="sends out a bundle this command did not make")
    r.add_argument("bundle", metavar="BUNDLE.age")
    r.add_argument("--prefix", default="platform", metavar="PREFIX",
                   help="the folder at the destination (default: platform, "
                        "which is where the STATE bundle belongs)")
    rsub.add_parser("list", help="what is at the destination, and how old")
    rsub.add_parser("status", help="the freshness of the destination, as metrics")
    r = rsub.add_parser("cadence", help="the clock: how often the backup runs")
    r.add_argument("--set", dest="cadence", metavar="DURATION", default=None,
                   help=f"{CADENCE_FLOOR_S // 3600}h..{CADENCE_CEILING_S // 3600}h "
                        f"freely; under the floor it needs a confirmation on "
                        f"the operator's phone")
    a = ap.parse_args()
    if a.verb == "remote":
        return remote_main(a)
    a.capture_dir   = a.directory if a.verb == "backup" else None
    a.list_bundle   = a.bundle if a.verb == "list" else None
    a.restore_bundle = a.bundle if a.verb == "restore" else None
    if a.verb != "restore":
        a.force = False
        a.from_remote = False
    if a.verb != "backup":
        a.no_remote = False
    if a.verb not in ("restore", "size"):
        a.org = None

    if a.verb == "size":
        return measure(a.org)
    if a.list_bundle:
        return list_bundle(a.list_bundle)
    if a.restore_bundle:
        if not a.from_remote:
            return restore(a.restore_bundle, a.org, a.force)
        # The bundle comes down FIRST and is checked against its
        # manifest before `restore` is even called: what the rest of
        # this file does with the pieces inside a bundle, this does with
        # the bundle itself. And it lands in a directory of ours that is
        # removed no matter how this ends — a downloaded copy left lying
        # around is a plaintext-adjacent artefact nobody remembers.
        down = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="aegis-remoto-")
        try:
            local = os.path.join(down, os.path.basename(a.restore_bundle))
            remote_fetch(a.restore_bundle, local)
            return restore(local, a.org, a.force)
        finally:
            shutil.rmtree(down, ignore_errors=True)

    return capture(a.capture_dir or DEFAULT_DEST, remote=not a.no_remote)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
